### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **“Automatic” pruning extent is not supported by a validity guarantee and is likely unstable across layers/datasets (Evidential)**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 3.4.1 (Kneedle on MSS over *k*), Algorithm 1 lines 7–12.  
   - **Why it matters:** The core claim is that ACSP “automatically determines layer-wise pruning levels” without manual tuning. But the paper provides no evidence that (i) the MSS–k curve has a well-defined knee in typical layers, (ii) the selected knee corresponds to an accuracy-preserving operating point, or (iii) the choice is robust to noise, dataset subsampling, or random seeds. A knee heuristic can easily pick very different k′ under small perturbations, especially when MSS changes smoothly or has multiple inflection points. Without a stability/robustness analysis, the “fully automated” claim is not established.

2) **The method’s central “complementary separation” mechanism is internally diluted by the final selection rule, and the paper does not show it is doing what is claimed (Structural)**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 3.3.2 (k-medoids to enforce diversity) vs. Sec. 3.4.2 (replace medoids by max-weight element per cluster); Algorithm 1 line 12 (“top-k′ components by weight”) is inconsistent with Sec. 3.4.2’s “largest weight from each cluster.”  
   - **Why it matters:** The headline idea is to keep components that are *diverse in separability space* (complementary). But the actual kept set depends heavily on weight magnitude (L1 norm / abs weight), which is a different criterion that can collapse the intended diversity (e.g., if high-weight channels concentrate in a few regions, you keep “diverse clusters” in name but pick points that are not representative of those clusters). Worse, Algorithm 1 suggests an even stronger override—simply taking global top-k′ by weight—contradicting the claimed per-cluster selection. As written, the method is not well-defined, and the “complementary selection” claim is not verifiable.

3) **Evaluation does not fairly isolate ACSP’s claimed contributions (Evidential)**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 4.1 (fine-tune after *each layer*; subset training), Table 1 comparisons, plus the claim “single pass per layer” in Sec. 1.  
   - **Why it matters:** Many baselines in Table 1 are reported from their papers under their own training/pruning recipes. ACSP uses a specific iterative layer-wise prune-with-intermediate-finetune schedule (2 epochs per layer on CIFAR, 3 on ImageNet, 25% subset). That schedule can itself change the final accuracy/speed tradeoff. Without re-running key baselines under the same iterative pruning/fine-tuning budget (or alternatively running ACSP under the baselines’ budgets), the comparisons do not attribute gains to (i) separability graph, (ii) clustering/MSS, or (iii) knee selection—as opposed to the prune+retrain procedure and hyperparameter choices.

4) **The separability graph construction is computationally and statistically problematic at scale, and the paper does not demonstrate it is practical beyond the tested class counts (Methodological gap)**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 3.3.1 (per-class-pair, per-pixel JM distances), Conclusion limitation.  
   - **Why it matters:** The approach scales with \(\binom{C}{2}\) and with spatial resolution \(p^2\). Even at ImageNet-1K, many intermediate feature maps have large \(p\), and the per-pixel per-class-pair estimation of means/variances is noisy unless class-conditional sample sizes are large. The paper acknowledges the class-pair scaling but does not quantify wall-clock overhead end-to-end (only Kneedle overhead) nor sensitivity to the subset size used to estimate JM statistics.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction
- The abstract claims “fully automated” pruning and “removing the need for manual tuning,” but Sec. 4.1 fixes specific fine-tuning schedules and subset fractions; the method may still require deployment-specific choices (how much data is available for activations, how much tuning budget per layer). This weakens the framing of “no manual tuning” in practice.
- The introduction claims “single pass per layer” and “no additional supervision or search” (Sec. 1). But Sec. 3.4 evaluates *all k from 2 to Ni* with k-medoids each time, which is a form of discrete search over k (albeit heuristic). That’s fine, but the rhetoric should match what is actually done.

### Related Work (Sec. 2)
- The positioning “existing methods do not explicitly enforce diversity among kept components” is plausible, but the paper does not discuss the closest conceptual relatives: approaches that encourage diversity/coverage (e.g., determinantal point processes, facility-location, submodular selection) which are the natural comparators to “complementary selection.” Even if not cited here, the paper needs to argue why k-medoids+MSS is the right diversity mechanism for pruning.

### Methodology (Sec. 3)
- **Definition/consistency issue:** Algorithm 1 line 12 (“top-k′ components by weight”) conflicts with Sec. 3.4.2 (pick max-weight component *within each cluster*). This is not a minor clarity issue; it changes the algorithm qualitatively.
- **Statistical estimation details missing:** For JM/Bhattacharyya distance (Eq. 1–2), the paper does not specify how it handles near-zero variances, whether it assumes Gaussian activations, whether it uses biased/unbiased variance, or any numerical stabilization. Those choices can dominate separability values.
- **Distance metric in clustering unspecified:** k-medoids requires a distance; the paper never states whether it is Euclidean/cosine/Manhattan on the separability vectors, nor any normalization/standardization. With vectors of dimension \(p^2\binom{C}{2}\), scale issues are severe.
- **Memory/compute realism:** Constructing explicit \(N_i \times (p^2 \binom{C}{2})\) matrices is enormous for early conv layers. The paper likely streams/aggregates rather than materializes full tensors, but it does not describe the implementation strategy. Given the centrality of “practical,” this omission matters.

### Experiments (Sec. 4)
- **Comparability:** Table 1 aggregates many methods across datasets/models, but without stating whether results are from the same training recipe, same base model checkpoints, same FLOP counting conventions, or same evaluation protocol. In pruning, these details are often outcome-determining.
- **Accuracy gains after pruning:** Several results show *improved* accuracy after pruning (e.g., ResNet-50 ImageNet +0.59). This can happen, but it raises the question of whether the reported “Base Accuracy” is under-trained relative to the fine-tuned pruned model (especially with repeated per-layer fine-tuning). The paper should verify by applying the same fine-tuning budget to the unpruned model as a control.
- **Latency vs FLOPs:** Table 2 shows modest latency reductions (often 3–10%) compared to large FLOP speed-ups (up to 2.6×). The paper acknowledges nonlinearity, but the gap is big enough that the “accelerating inference time” claim should be anchored in latency as the primary metric, not FLOPs. As is, the best-case FLOP ratios may overstate real deployment benefits.

### Conclusion (Sec. 5)
- The stated limitation (class-pair scaling) is real, but the paper does not connect it back to what practitioners should do (e.g., recommended sampling strategy) nor demonstrate that accuracy is preserved when approximations are used.

## Strengths
- Clear high-level motivation: automating pruning extent selection and encouraging non-redundant retained components.
- Uses supervised information explicitly via class-pair separability rather than purely weight magnitude, which is a reasonable direction for preserving discriminative capacity.
- Broad architectural coverage in experiments (VGG/ResNet/DenseNet/MobileNet, CIFAR and ImageNet) and includes actual latency measurements (Table 2), not only FLOPs.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Stability of the automatic k′ choice:** rerun ACSP with multiple random seeds and with different activation-estimation subsets; report variance of chosen k′ per layer and resulting accuracy/FLOPs. Without this, “automatic” is not convincing.  
2. **Controls for fine-tuning budget:** apply the same per-layer fine-tuning schedule to (i) an unpruned model (“extra training” control) and (ii) a simple baseline pruning rule (e.g., L1-norm structured pruning with the same automatically chosen k′) to attribute gains to the separability+diversity mechanism.  
3. **Ablation of the complementarity mechanism:** compare (a) global top-k′ by weight, (b) k-medoids medoids, (c) per-cluster max-weight (claimed), (d) random-per-cluster; show accuracy vs FLOPs and show whether diversity actually matters.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Demonstrate “complementary separation” quantitatively:** report redundancy/diversity metrics of retained components (e.g., average pairwise distance in separability space, coverage) and correlate with accuracy retention. Right now it is an appealing story without verification.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Layer-wise MSS curves and chosen knees:** show representative MSS–k plots for different layers/models and mark the chosen k′. This is essential to justify Kneedle as the automation mechanism.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Approximate class-pair computation:** implement and evaluate class-pair sampling or low-rank embeddings of separability vectors, showing the accuracy/compute tradeoff; otherwise scalability remains speculative.

## Overall Assessment
The paper’s core idea—selecting diverse, discriminative components via a supervised separability space and using a knee heuristic to automate pruning extent—is plausible, and the empirical results are promising. However, as submitted, the work does not yet support its central claims because the algorithm is not internally consistent about how components are ultimately selected (which directly impacts whether “complementary selection” is actually being performed), and the evaluation does not isolate ACSP’s claimed contributions from its iterative fine-tuning procedure and other recipe choices. Given these issues, I do not think the paper is ready for acceptance in its current form.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Fully automatic, per-layer pruning volume selection with a concrete decision rule**: ACSP evaluates all subset sizes \(k\in\{2,\dots,N_i\}\) per layer via k-Medoids + MSS scoring, then chooses the pruning extent using Kneedle on the MSS curve (Sec. 3.2, Sec. 3.4.1; Algorithm 1 lines 7–12), directly addressing the “manual pruning ratio” limitation that many structured/activation methods retain (Sec. 1–2).
2. **Complementarity/diversity is operationalized (not just claimed) via graph-space clustering and MSS**: Components are embedded into a “separation graph space” where proximity corresponds to similar class-pair separability, then k-Medoids is used explicitly to pick representatives from different regions (Sec. 3.3.2; Fig. 2), and MSS is chosen specifically because it measures separation to *all* other clusters (not only the nearest), matching the stated redundancy-minimization goal (Sec. “Mean Simplified Silhouette”).
3. **Supervised, class-pair separability signal is computed at the component level and supports conv + linear layers**: The paper defines a layer-wise representation where each component gets a vector over all class pairs (and spatial positions for conv layers), using Jeffries–Matusita distance (Eq. (1) with Bhattacharyya form immediately below; Sec. 3.3.1), enabling a consistent selection criterion across layer types—a more task-aligned signal than magnitude-only structured pruning.
4. **Strong empirical evidence for compute reduction with competitive accuracy vs. multiple pruning baselines across architectures/datasets**: Table 1 shows ACSP achieving top speed-ups while maintaining/improving accuracy on (i) CIFAR-10 MobileNet-V2 (94.98%, +0.50, **1.93×**), (ii) CIFAR-10 VGG-16 (+0.37, **2.59×**), (iii) CIFAR-10 ResNet-56 (**2.15×** with +0.13), and (iv) ImageNet ResNet-50 (**2.25×** with +0.59), demonstrating the “1.5–2.5× FLOPs reduction without accuracy loss” claim in a cross-model setting (Sec. 4.2–4.4; Table 1).

### Supporting strengths
1. **Bridges FLOP reductions to real latency gains with a dedicated timing study**: Beyond FLOP-based “Speed Up” in Table 1, the paper measures batch and single-sample inference latency on GPU with warm-up and 100-run averaging (Sec. 4.5), showing consistent wall-clock reductions (e.g., CIFAR-10 MobileNet-V2 batch −20.39%; ImageNet ResNet-50 single −8.07%; Table 2), which many pruning papers omit or report less systematically.
2. **Practical component choice combines diversity with within-cluster importance**: After picking \(k\) via MSS/Kneedle and clustering, ACSP selects the highest-weight element per cluster (L1-norm for conv filters; absolute weight for FC) to avoid ignoring magnitude-based importance (Sec. 3.4.2; Fig. 2), a concrete mechanism to reconcile “coverage” with “salience.”
3. **Method flexibility is demonstrated (not just asserted) for the separability metric**: The paper reports it evaluated alternative distances (Hellinger, Wasserstein) and chose JM because it gave the best performance/efficiency balance (end of Sec. 3.3.1), strengthening robustness of the core “separability-embedding” design.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Algorithmic clarity with end-to-end procedure and complexity note**: Algorithm 1 specifies the exact per-layer routine (activation extraction → graph space → k sweep → MSS → Kneedle → prune → brief fine-tune), and Sec. 3.2 quantifies the Kneedle runtime and gives a concrete overhead estimate for \(N_i\le 256\) (<0.1s on RTX 6000), aiding reproducibility and deployment planning.

## Summary

The paper proposes ACSP, a pruning method that automatically decides *how much* to prune per layer while explicitly enforcing *complementary* (non-redundant) retained components using a supervised class-pair separability embedding plus clustering/MSS and Kneedle (Sec. 3; Algorithm 1). The strongest evidence that it delivers on its efficiency-without-accuracy-loss goal is Table 1’s consistent cross-architecture results showing large FLOP speed-ups (e.g., **2.25×** on ImageNet ResNet-50) paired with maintained or improved accuracy (+0.59), complemented by real inference-time reductions reported in Table 2.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper proposes **Automatic Complementary Separation Pruning (ACSP)**, a supervised structured pruning method that (i) embeds each channel/neuron into a “separability graph space” using class-pair Jeffries–Matusita distances computed from activations, (ii) sweeps \(k=2..N_i\) with k-medoids and MSS to score diversity/coverage, and (iii) uses Kneedle on the MSS–\(k\) curve to automatically pick a per-layer pruning extent, with light fine-tuning after each layer. Experiments report 1.5–2.6× FLOP reductions with small accuracy changes and modest measured latency improvements.

## Strengths
- **Clear, concrete pipeline for automatic per-layer pruning extent selection** (k sweep → MSS → Kneedle; Algorithm 1 lines 7–12; Sec. 3.2, 3.4.1), targeting the “manual pruning ratio” pain point.
- **Broad empirical coverage with both FLOPs and measured latency**: Table 1 covers multiple architectures/datasets; Table 2 reports batch + single-sample timing with a stated measurement protocol (Sec. 4.5).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **Algorithm is internally inconsistent about what is actually selected after determining \(k'\)**, which makes the central “complementary selection” mechanism hard to verify and potentially not what is implemented. Specifically, Sec. 3.3.2 says k-medoids selects **medoids** as the complementary set; Sec. 3.4.2 instead says to select the **largest-weight element within each cluster**; but Algorithm 1 line 12 says **“optimal components ← top-\(k'\) components by weight”** (global ranking), which can ignore clustering entirely. These are qualitatively different procedures and can change results substantially.
- **The “fully automated / no search” framing is overstated relative to the actual procedure.** The method explicitly evaluates **all \(k\in\{2,\dots,N_i\}\)** and runs k-medoids + MSS for each (Algorithm 1 lines 7–10; Sec. 3.4.1), i.e., a discrete sweep over \(k\). That can still be reasonable, but the claim “single pass per layer … without … search” (Intro) does not match the described computation.
- **Comparisons in Table 1 do not isolate ACSP’s claimed contribution from the chosen fine-tuning recipe**, making attribution unclear. The paper’s protocol fine-tunes **after each pruned layer** for fixed epochs on a **25% subset** (Sec. 4.1). Since Table 1 aggregates many baselines that may use different retraining/pruning schedules, it is hard to conclude improvements come from the separability+complementarity criterion rather than the iterative prune+per-layer-finetune recipe.

### Minor
- **Key implementation choices that materially affect the method are unspecified**: k-medoids requires a distance in the separability space, but the paper does not state the metric or any normalization/standardization of the very high-dimensional separability vectors (Sec. 3.3.2). This is not a “hyperparameter nitpick”: distance choice can change clustering structure and thus pruning decisions.
- **Compute/practicality of graph-space construction is not quantified end-to-end.** The paper notes Kneedle overhead is small (Sec. 3.2), and acknowledges class-pair scaling as a limitation (Sec. 5), but it does not report the runtime/memory cost of computing per-class-pair (and per-pixel for conv layers) JM statistics, nor sensitivity to the 25% subset size used to estimate them.

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves
- Add a small stability study of the **chosen \(k'\)** (variance across random subsets/seeds) and show a few representative MSS–\(k\) curves with the selected knee to make the “automatic extent” behavior more transparent.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“Statistical estimation details missing for JM (near-zero variances, Gaussian assumptions, biased/unbiased variance)”**: the paper defines JM via class-conditional means/variances (Sec. 3.3.1, Eq. 1–2) and it’s reasonable for a pruning paper not to enumerate every numerical stabilization choice; this is better framed as a minor reproducibility detail unless evidence shows instability.
- **“Memory/compute realism: explicit \(N_i\times(p^2\binom{C}{2})\) is enormous so likely impossible”**: while scalability concerns are valid (kept above as Minor), asserting the implementation must materialize the full tensor is speculative; the paper could compute statistics streaming/aggregated.

## Novel Insights
The most consequential issue is not the knee heuristic itself, but that the paper currently has **three different, incompatible definitions of the retained set** (medoids vs per-cluster max-weight vs global top-\(k'\) by weight). This undermines interpretability of both the “complementary separation” story and the empirical results because the reader cannot tell which mechanism is responsible for performance.

## Suggestions
- Make the algorithm **unambiguous**: reconcile Algorithm 1 line 12 with Sec. 3.4.2 (and Sec. 3.3.2) and state precisely whether selection is (a) medoids, (b) max-weight-within-each-cluster, or (c) global top-\(k'\) by weight.
- Add a **matched-budget control**: run a simple magnitude-based structured baseline under the *same* per-layer fine-tuning schedule (Sec. 4.1), and/or run ACSP under a baseline’s retraining budget, to clarify attribution.
- Specify the **distance metric and normalization** used for k-medoids in separability space, and report approximate **end-to-end pruning-time overhead** (graph construction + k sweep) for at least ImageNet/ResNet-50.

## Score and Decision
**Calibration note:** I could not run `calibration_search` due to a tool error (“invalid model ID”), so I cannot provide the required anchored comparison list across >7 / 4–6 / <3 bands. Given that limitation, my score is based only on the paper text and typical ICLR standards.

ACSP targets an important practical problem and shows promising empirical numbers, but the **method definition inconsistency** plus **unclear attribution vs the fine-tuning recipe** are major issues for acceptance at ICLR without clarification/extra experiments.

