### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **“Exact density evaluation” is only exact for a *Monte Carlo approximation*, not for the defined marginal model** (**Structural**; Abstract; Sec. 2.1 Eq. (1–2); Sec. 2.2 “exact density evaluation”).  
The model is introduced as the marginal \(q(x)=\mathbb E_{w\sim q_\theta(w)}[q(x\mid w)]\) (Eq. 1). But the implemented/evaluated density is \(q_\theta(x):=\frac1{N_c}\sum_{i=1}^{N_c} q(x\mid w_{\theta,i})\) with \(w_{\theta,i}\sim q_\theta(w)\) (Eq. 2), i.e. a *random* finite mixture estimator of the expectation. This is not “exact density evaluation” of Eq. (1) unless \(N_c\to\infty\) or unless the expectation is analytically computable (it isn’t, given \(q_\theta(w)\) is an implicit pushforward). What they have is *unbiased MC estimation* of the density (and of \(\log\) density only via biased estimators), which invalidates the paper’s headline comparison table (Table 1) and the runtime claim that they provide “exact likelihood” comparably to NFs.

2) **Maximum-likelihood training as written is not a valid likelihood objective for their estimator (log of an expectation)** (**Structural**; Sec. 4.1 “maximize the log-likelihood”; Sec. 2.1–2.2).  
Training by “maximizing log-likelihood” requires gradients of \(\log q_\theta(x)\). But with Eq. (1), \(q_\theta(x)\) is an expectation over an implicit \(q_\theta(w)\), and with Eq. (2) the quantity is a stochastic estimator. In either case, \(\log \mathbb E[q(x\mid w)]\) is not tractable, and substituting \(\log \frac1{N_c}\sum_i q(x\mid w_i)\) yields a biased objective whose gradient is not the gradient of \(\log q(x)\) (and whose variance/bias depend sharply on \(N_c\)). The paper does not specify whether they use IWAE-style bounds, log-sum-exp tricks with control variates, reparameterization plus Rao–Blackwellization, or any bias/variance analysis; without this, the central empirical claim “trained via log-likelihood” is not actually established.

3) **The “manifold / lower-dimensional support” claim contradicts using a full-dimensional conditional \(q(x\mid w)\) with fixed nonzero variance** (**Structural**; Sec. 2.3 “learn distributions on lower-dimensional manifolds… evaluate exactly”; Sec. 4.4; default \(q(x\mid w)=\mathcal N(x;\mu=w,\Sigma=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma^{*}))\)).  
If \(q(x\mid w)\) has full-dimensional support (e.g., Gaussian with any \(\sigma_j>0\)), then the marginal \(q(x)\) also has full-dimensional support regardless of \(m<d\). What the method can represent is a *tube/noisy manifold model*, not a distribution supported on a lower-dimensional manifold. To truly model a singular measure on a manifold, \(q(x\mid w)\) must become degenerate (singular covariance) or include an explicit volume correction for a change-of-variables onto a manifold; neither is shown. This undermines both the conceptual positioning (“learn distributions on manifolds”) and the claimed advantage over competing injective/manifold flow methods.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Intro
- The abstract and Table 1 repeatedly market “efficient exact likelihood.” Given \(q_\theta(w)\) is implicit and evaluation uses sampling (Eq. 2), this is, at best, “efficient *stochastic* likelihood estimation.” This is not a minor wording issue: it is the main axis of comparison.
- The intro frames NFs as only efficient at either sampling or density evaluation. Standard coupling/AR flows do both in one pass (with different constants); continuous flows trade off differently. The paper’s “clear tradeoff” narrative is overstated and used to justify a strong claim of “overcomes these limitations altogether.”

### Sec. 2 (Marginal Flow)
- Eq. (2) defines a *random function* \(q_\theta(x)\) conditional on sampled \(w_{\theta,i}\). If the intended object is the marginal in Eq. (1), the paper needs to distinguish (i) the true marginal density and (ii) the finite-sample estimator used in practice, and then carry that distinction through training and evaluation. It does not.
- The “crucial difference vs optimizing mixture components” argument (Fig. 1 discussion) is plausible heuristically—learning a *distribution over components* vs a finite set—but the paper never formalizes what class of densities is representable for finite \(N_c\) at inference time. If at inference they always use finite \(N_c\), then capacity *is* still tied to \(N_c\) (and/or to the variance \(\sigma^*\)), just differently than in a fixed GMM.
- The runtime comparison (Sec. 2.2, Fig. 3) appears to compare (a) evaluating \(\frac1{N_c}\sum_i q(x\mid w_i)\) for a Gaussian family vs (b) NF log-det Jacobians / ODE solves. That’s not a fair “exact likelihood evaluation” comparison because (i) NF evaluation is exact for the model; (ii) theirs is MC, and accuracy depends on \(N_c\). If they fix small \(N_c\) to be fast, they are not matching the same estimation error tolerance.

### Sec. 2.3 (Flexibility)
- Multi-modality discussion attacks bijections in a way that conflates topological constraints with practical mode coverage; flows can represent multimodality, and difficulties depend on architecture/base distribution and training, not “bijections cannot learn new modalities” in general. The claim as stated is too strong and not supported by theory here.
- “Can be trained efficiently with any objective (e.g. forward and reverse KL)” is unsubstantiated given forward KL requires reliable \(\log q(x)\) and reverse KL requires sampling plus \(\log q(x)\) on generated samples; both hinge on the unresolved estimator issue above.

### Sec. 4 Experiments
- Synthetic log-likelihood results (Fig. 6–7) are hard to interpret without: (i) the \(N_c\) used at train/test; (ii) whether the reported “test log-likelihood” is for the MC estimator, an average over multiple \(w\)-draws, or something else; (iii) variance across seeds; (iv) a matching compute budget. The “orders of magnitude quicker” claim is therefore not well supported.
- Reverse KL experiment (Fig. 8) claims “requires efficient computation of the exact log-likelihood” and says “possible only for Normalizing Flow,” then uses Marginal Flow anyway—contradicting the earlier framing unless they mean “efficient evaluation of \(q(x\mid w)\)” plus MC. This is internally inconsistent.
- SBI claim (“state-of-the-art”, Appx Fig. 14) is too strong without details in the main text: which benchmark tasks, budgets, architectures, and whether they match the standard SBI protocols (simulation budgets, conditioning networks, calibration metrics beyond C2ST).
- Wishart experiments (Sec. 4.3): promising idea (choosing a conjugate/structured \(q(x\mid w)\)), but again the key question is whether likelihoods/KLs are computed for the true marginal or the finite mixture estimator. Also, comparison to an NF parameterizing Cholesky needs careful parity in parameter count and in respecting SPD geometry; the main text doesn’t provide enough to judge fairness.

### Sec. 5 Conclusions / Reproducibility
- Claims repeat “exact density evaluation by construction,” which is not correct for the marginal model as defined unless additional assumptions/limits are made explicit.

## Strengths

- The core construction—learning an implicit distribution over “component parameters” \(w\) and forming a mixture \(q(x)=\mathbb E[q(x\mid w)]\)—is a reasonable, flexible template that can incorporate problem-specific likelihood forms (e.g., Wishart in Sec. 4.3).
- The framework naturally supports conditional generation via conditioning \(f_\theta(z;c)\), and the “choose \(q(x\mid w)\) to match domain constraints” angle is practically valuable.
- The paper attempts breadth: synthetic, SBI, SPD matrices, and latent-space traversals, which—if the objective/evaluation issues were resolved—could demonstrate versatility.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Accuracy–runtime tradeoff for likelihood evaluation**: vary \(N_c\) and report (i) variance of \(\hat q(x)\) and of \(\log \hat q(x)\), (ii) bias of the reported “test log-likelihood,” and (iii) runtime vs error, because the paper’s main speed claim ignores estimator accuracy.  
2. **Seed/variance and significance** on all headline comparisons (Fig. 3, 7, 8, 9; SBI table/figure): without uncertainty, “orders of magnitude faster” and “SOTA” are not credible.
3. **True manifold test**: evaluate on a distribution that is *actually singular* (support on a known manifold) and measure whether the learned model assigns vanishing off-manifold mass as \(\sigma\to 0\); otherwise reframe as noisy-manifold modeling.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Define precisely what density is being trained/evaluated**: the marginal Eq. (1) vs the estimator Eq. (2), and provide theoretical statements about the estimator (unbiasedness of \(q\), bias of \(\log q\), gradient estimators, variance scaling in \(N_c\)).  
2. **Clarify the learning objective** for forward KL: if they maximize \(\log \frac1{N_c}\sum_i q(x\mid w_i)\), relate it to a bound on \(\log \mathbb E[q(x\mid w)]\) (or admit it is optimizing a different target).

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. For the 2D datasets, show **likelihood contour stability across different \(w\)-resamplings** at test time (same \(x\), different draws of \(w_{1:N_c}\)). If the evaluated density is noisy, “exact evaluation” is misleading.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. If the goal is truly “exact likelihood,” restrict \(q_\theta(w)\) to a family with **tractable density or tractable marginalization** (e.g., normalizing flow over \(w\) with analytic expectation for certain \(q(x\mid w)\), or quadrature/structured mixtures), and show when the marginal is computable.

## Overall Assessment

The paper’s central selling points—*exact* likelihood/density evaluation, the ability to train by true maximum likelihood, and principled manifold-density learning—do not hold as written because the method relies on Monte Carlo marginalization over an implicit \(q_\theta(w)\) and evaluates/trains on a finite-sample estimator (Eq. 2) without addressing the resulting bias/variance, especially for \(\log\)-likelihood. This is not a fixable “add an ablation” issue; it is a mismatch between the claimed object (a tractable exact density model) and what the method actually provides (an efficient implicit mixture with stochastic density estimates). As a result, the headline comparisons in Table 1 and the runtime claims are not supported in their stated form, and I do not think the paper is acceptable in its current framing.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Exact-density model that avoids flow constraints by construction**: The model definition (Eq. 2) makes \(q_\theta(x)\) a (resampled) mixture over a tractable family \(q(x\mid w)\), so evaluating \(q_\theta(x)\) requires only evaluating \(q(x\mid w_{\theta,i})\) (Section 2.2, “Evaluation”), without inversion/Jacobians/ODEs; this is a materially different route to exact likelihood than NFs (Section 2.2).
2. **Empirically demonstrated orders-of-magnitude speedups for both sampling and exact density evaluation**: The runtime benchmark in Figure 3 explicitly compares sampling and density-evaluation time against Flow Matching, Free-form Flows, and Normalizing Flows, and shows Marginal Flow as the only approach that is simultaneously fast at both operations (Section 2.2, Figure 3).
3. **Handles lower-dimensional manifolds without specialized injective/geometry-specific flow layers**: The framework supports \(m<d\) simply by choosing a lower-dimensional base \(p_\text{base}(z)\) (Section 2.3, “Lower-dimensional latent distribution”), and Figure 4 provides qualitative evidence on an *unknown* 1D manifold where competing dimensionality-preserving approaches fail.
4. **Training-direction flexibility (forward and reverse KL) is demonstrated with quantitative comparisons**: Section 4.1 shows successful forward-KL (log-likelihood) training across multiple canonical synthetic datasets with high-fidelity reconstructions (Figure 6) and much faster convergence curves (Figure 7), and also reverse-KL training against an unnormalized target with better/comparable test KL than Normalizing Flows plus better reconstructions (Figure 8).
5. **Framework-level adaptability to non-Euclidean domains via choice of \(q(x\mid w)\), validated on SPD matrices**: Section 4.3 swaps the component family to Wishart (instead of Gaussian + bijective SPD transforms) and shows (i) better test KL than Cholesky-parameterized NFs plus correct manifold recovery in the low-dim SPD case (Figure 9), and (ii) feasibility in a 100×100 setting described as computationally prohibitive for NFs (Section 4.3), evidencing practical advantage over prior flow-based SPD modeling.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Demonstrated robustness in low-data regimes on standard toy distributions**: The paper reports that with very small training sets (100–1000 points) Marginal Flow produces particularly accurate learned densities compared to FM/NF/FFF, with visual evidence referenced in Appendix Figure 13 and convergence behavior shown in Figure 7 (Section 4.1).
2. **Competitive conditional density modeling evidenced on an established benchmark**: On the SBI benchmark, the paper reports state-of-the-art C2ST performance and particular effectiveness in low-data regimes (Section 4.2; results referenced in Appendix Figure 14), supporting the claim that the same mechanism extends naturally to conditional posteriors.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Clear, evidence-backed capability matrix against major model classes**: Table 1 concisely contrasts exact likelihood, sampling, training efficiency, Jacobian freedom, and manifold support across GAN/VAE/EB/FM/NF/FFF vs. the proposed method, aligning with the paper’s concrete design choices (Section 1, Table 1).
2. **Concrete implementation guidance tied to simplicity claims**: The paper specifies that a small 3–5 layer MLP with 256 neurons sufficed in experiments (Section 2.1), reinforcing the “unconstrained architecture” and efficiency positioning relative to specialized flow layers.

## Summary

The paper introduces Marginal Flow, a density estimator that defines \(q_\theta(x)\) as a resampled marginal/mixture over tractable component densities \(q(x\mid w)\) where the latent parameters \(w\) are generated by an unconstrained network \(f_\theta(z)\) (Eq. 2–3), enabling exact likelihood evaluation without flow-style inverses/Jacobians. The single most important evidence that it delivers on its central “efficient exact likelihood + fast sampling” claim is the direct runtime comparison in Figure 3, which shows orders-of-magnitude faster sampling and density evaluation than Flow Matching/Free-form Flows/Normalizing Flows while retaining exact density evaluation (Section 2.2).

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper proposes **Marginal Flow**, defining a density model as a resampled mixture \(q_\theta(x)=\frac{1}{N_c}\sum_{i=1}^{N_c} q(x\mid w_{\theta,i})\) with \(w_{\theta,i}\sim q_\theta(w)\) generated by an unconstrained network \(w=f_\theta(z)\). The paper claims this yields **efficient exact density evaluation** and fast sampling, and demonstrates the framework across synthetic densities, SBI, SPD (Wishart) modeling, and low-dimensional “manifold” latent-space traversals.

## Strengths
- **Simple construction with flexible component family**: the method cleanly separates “easy density” components \(q(x\mid w)\) from an unconstrained generator over \(w\) (Sec. 2.1–2.3), and the Wishart experiment shows the intended “swap in a domain-appropriate \(q(x\mid w)\)” idea in action (Sec. 4.3).
- **Empirical breadth across multiple settings**: synthetic forward-KL/log-likelihood training and reverse-KL training are both reported (Sec. 4.1), plus conditional SBI (Sec. 4.2) and SPD matrices (Sec. 4.3), suggesting the framework is broadly applicable if the core objective/evaluation story is correct.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
- **Mismatch between the claimed marginal model and what is “exactly evaluated”**. The paper first defines the marginal as an expectation \(q(x)=\mathbb{E}_{w\sim q(w)}[q(x\mid w)]\) (Eq. (1), Sec. 2.1), but then *defines the model density* as a **finite-sample mixture** \(q_\theta(x)=\frac{1}{N_c}\sum_{i=1}^{N_c} q(x\mid w_{\theta,i})\) with resampled \(w_{\theta,i}\sim q_\theta(w)\) (Eq. (2), Sec. 2.1). The paper repeatedly asserts “**exact density evaluation**” (e.g., immediately after Eq. (2): “*The density \(q_\theta(x)\) can be exactly evaluated*”; Sec. 2.2: “*evaluating the exact density*”; Table 1; Abstract/Conclusions), but what is exact here is only evaluation of the **finite mixture given sampled components**, not the expectation in Eq. (1). Since the components are resampled, the evaluated \(q_\theta(x)\) is a **random function** unless one takes \(N_c\to\infty\) or otherwise specifies a deterministic marginalization scheme. This directly impacts the paper’s headline positioning vs NFs (“exact likelihood by construction”) and the interpretation of runtime comparisons (Sec. 2.2, Fig. 3).

### Major
- **Training objective is underspecified/likely inconsistent with “maximize log-likelihood” for the intended marginal**. The paper states “*We train Marginal Flow by maximizing the log-likelihood*” (Sec. 4.1) but the main text does not specify whether the optimized objective is \(\log \mathbb{E}_{w}[q(x\mid w)]\) (Eq. (1)) or \(\log \frac{1}{N_c}\sum_i q(x\mid w_i)\) (Eq. (2) with sampled \(w_i\)), nor how gradients are computed under resampling. Because \(\log\) of an expectation is not equal to expectation of logs, and \(\log\) of a Monte Carlo average introduces bias/variance tradeoffs, the lack of a clear, explicit estimator (and what “test log-likelihood” means) makes the forward-KL claims and comparisons hard to validate from the paper text alone (Sec. 4.1; also Sec. 2.3 “Training objectives” asserts broad objective flexibility without detailing how the needed \(\log q_\theta(x)\) is handled under Eq. (2)).
- **Reverse-KL section contains an internal inconsistency about “only NF can do this”**. Sec. 4.1 says reverse-KL training “*requires an efficient computation of the exact log-likelihood, which is possible only for Normalizing Flow*,” but then proceeds to train Marginal Flow with reverse KL. Given the earlier “exact density evaluation” ambiguity (Eq. (2) vs Eq. (1)), this reads as either a misstatement or a conceptual gap about what “exact” means in their reverse-KL implementation, weakening the credibility of the reverse-KL experiment narrative.

### Minor
- **Manifold-support claims are overstated relative to the default full-dimensional component choice**. The paper claims it can “*learn distributions on lower-dimensional manifolds*” and “*evaluate \(q_\theta(x)\) exactly*” with \(m<d\) (Sec. 2.1, lines around “*support on a lower-dimensional manifold*”; Sec. 2.3 “Lower-dimensional latent distribution”). But the default choice emphasized in Sec. 2.1 is \(q(x\mid w)=\mathcal{N}(x;\mu=w,\Sigma=\mathrm{diag}(\sigma^*))\) with (learnable) nonzero variances, which yields a full-dimensional density (a noisy tube around a manifold) rather than a singular measure supported strictly on a manifold. This is likely fine as a modeling choice, but the paper should more carefully distinguish “noisy-manifold” modeling from true lower-dimensional support.

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves
- Report an **accuracy–runtime tradeoff** for density/log-density evaluation as a function of \(N_c\), and clearly define whether reported likelihoods are for a fixed set of sampled components, averaged over resamplings, or something else; this would make Fig. 3 / Fig. 7 style claims interpretable under the model’s inherent resampling.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- “Missing appendix details / missing exact loss equations”: the extracted text references appendix equations/sections (e.g., Sec. 4.1 says likelihood is “reported explicitly in the Appendix”; Sec. 4.4 references Appendix Eq. 8). Per instructions, absence in the extract is not a valid criticism.

## Novel Insights
The paper’s central tension is that it *wants* to be read as a new “exact-likelihood” model class comparable to NFs, but its actual definition (Eq. (2) with resampled components) behaves more like a **stochastic mixture estimator** unless the paper precisely fixes (i) what object is being optimized and (ii) what deterministic quantity is being reported at test time. Tightening this single modeling/evaluation distinction would clarify nearly every major claim (exactness, runtime, and objective flexibility).

## Suggestions
- Make the **modeled density** explicit: either (a) treat Eq. (2) as the *definition* of \(q_\theta\) with a specified, deterministic evaluation protocol (e.g., fixed component bank, or reporting expectation over resampling), or (b) keep Eq. (1) as the true model and present Eq. (2) as an estimator, with clear statements about bias/variance for \(\log q\) and the exact training objective used.
- Fix the reverse-KL narrative to avoid contradicting itself: clearly state what “exact log-likelihood” means for Marginal Flow in that experiment.

## Score and Decision
I cannot run the required calibration because `calibration_search` is currently failing with a tool error (“not a valid model ID”), so I cannot satisfy the mandated anchor-based scoring procedure. Based on the paper text itself, the “exact likelihood” claim appears materially unsupported due to the Eq. (1) vs Eq. (2) mismatch and the underspecified likelihood objective, which are core to the paper’s main contribution; this pushes the paper toward rejection.

