### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1. **Structural — Evaluation uses the model’s own (noisy) SG extractor as “ground truth,” so the main accuracy claims are not well-identified.**  
   **Where:** Sec. 3.3 (CompSGen Bench); Sec. 3.1 (annotations from GPT‑4o); Tables 2–4 (SG‑IoU / Entity‑IoU / Relation‑IoU).  
   The benchmark’s “GT” scene graphs are themselves automatically produced (GPT‑4o) and only *partially* verified. Then the paper evaluates generations by comparing (i) an extracted SG from the generated image to (ii) this auto-SG annotation. This creates two coupled failure modes that can inflate improvements without real semantic gains: (a) systematic biases in the annotator/extractor (e.g., over-predicting common relations like “surrounded by”) become the target; (b) models trained on LAION‑Comp can learn to match those biases rather than the image. Without an evaluation anchored to independent human judgments or independently annotated GT graphs on the benchmark subset, the central claim “significantly better compositional correctness” is not actually established—only “better agreement with our auto-annotation pipeline.”

2. **Evidential — The headline conclusion “data, not architecture, is the core bottleneck” is not supported by the experimental design.**  
   **Where:** Introduction (claim that prior architectural work “failed fundamentally” because the limitation is the dataset); Sec. 5.1 (comparisons); Table 2 (cross-dataset training).  
   The paper changes *both* data (LAION‑Comp vs COCO/VG) and modeling (their SG encoder design + integration + fine-tuning of large backbones). Even Table 2’s “same model trained on different datasets” comparisons are confounded by distribution shifts: LAION‑Comp images are LAION‑Aesthetics (high aesthetic, web-scale, different content) whereas COCO/VG are captioning datasets with different domains. As a result, higher scores can come from domain match to CompSGen Bench (which is sampled from LAION‑Comp) rather than “structural annotation quality/scale” per se. The paper needs a design that isolates annotation structure from domain (e.g., same images with caption-only vs SG conditions; or benchmark drawn from an external dataset with human SGs). As written, the strong causal narrative (“architecture doesn’t matter; data does”) is overstated.

3. **Methodological gap (but important) — Benchmark construction is in-domain and may largely measure memorization / distribution matching rather than compositional generalization.**  
   **Where:** Sec. 3.3 (CompSGen Bench sampled from LAION‑Comp test split); Sec. 5.1 (main results on CompSGen Bench).  
   Because CompSGen Bench is drawn from the same curated LAION‑Aesthetics subset and annotated in the same way as training data, it is not a stress test of compositional *generalization*; it is primarily an in-distribution test of reproducing LAION‑Comp’s representation. This is fine for showing “we can fit the dataset,” but weaker for the paper’s broader claim of “unlocking controllable and compositional generation” as a generally better paradigm. The single sentence that they also evaluate T2I‑CompBench (Sec. A.6) is not enough in the main paper to carry the generalization claim.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction (Sec. 1)
- The framing draws a hard line that the “critical limitation” is *not architecture* but “fundamental deficiency” in datasets. The experiments do not justify such exclusivity; at best they show structured conditioning + large-scale structured data helps.
- The paper claims SGs are “more compact” than text, but Table 1 reports longer “length” for SG (32.2 vs 19.0). This isn’t fatal (structure can be more *unambiguous*), but the argument should be stated carefully: the advantage is explicit alignment of attributes/relations to entities, not compactness.

### Related Work (Sec. 2)
- Generally fine; it cites both controllable T2I and SG2IM lines. The review could be more careful in not implying prior work “failed fundamentally” without specifying what “failure” means (e.g., which benchmarks/metrics).

### Dataset & Benchmark (Sec. 3)
- **Sec. 3.1 annotation reliability:** reporting 98.8/97.5/95.7% accuracy from “partial human verification” is too underspecified to be persuasive. Key missing details that affect whether the dataset is trustworthy: sampling scheme (random? stratified by complexity?), number of annotators, definition of correctness for open-vocabulary relations/attributes, and inter-annotator agreement. (You say details are in Sec. A.5, but the main paper leans heavily on these numbers.)
- **Table 1 / Fig. 3 claims:** “SG is more accurate than caption” uses SG-IoU+/Entity-IoU+/Relation-IoU+ metrics (Sec. A.2) that likely depend on an automated SG parsing/extraction step. If so, this is again self-referential: captions may look worse because the evaluation tool is better aligned to SG format than free text.
- **Sec. 3.3 metrics:** The CLIP score is described as “similarity between generated and ground truth images,” which is an unusual use (CLIP is typically image–text or embedding similarity with caveats). If it is image–image CLIP similarity, it is not standard and needs justification since it rewards reproducing dataset style/content and may penalize legitimate variations.

### Foundation Models (Sec. 4)
- The method description is plausible (CLIP-encoded triples + GNN refinement + scaling α). But the integration into SDXL/SD3.5/FLUX is not described enough in the main text to evaluate fairness vs SG-Adapter/SGDiff. Given that SG-Adapter is an attention-based conditioning method, small implementation choices can dominate outcomes; relegating most of this to Sec. A.9 makes it hard to assess whether improvements come from LAION‑Comp or from a stronger conditioning/integration pipeline.
- Treating each relation word as separate edges (“if relation contains multiple words…”) may produce artifacts (e.g., “in front of” becomes multiple edges with different semantics). This could matter for relation correctness, and the paper doesn’t discuss how it normalizes multiword relations.

### Experiments (Sec. 5)
- **Table 2 (cross-dataset training):** The comparison “train SG-Adapter / SDXL-SG on COCO/VG/LAION‑Comp” is informative but still conflates dataset domain with annotation benefits. Also, the table mixes T2I models (trained on LAION) with SG2IM models trained on various datasets; readers could misinterpret the results as showing “SG beats text” when the supervision and datasets differ.
- **Table 3 (CompSGen Bench):** Strong gains in SG-IoU etc. are exactly where the self-referential benchmark concern bites. Without an external/human evaluation of relational correctness on a subset, it’s not clear the metric corresponds to what humans perceive as correct composition.
- The paper asserts “for fairness, we train SGDiff without bbox” (Sec. 5.1). That helps, but it may also disadvantage SGDiff if its design expects boxes; fairness requires re-tuning and reporting whether the “no bbox” variant is a meaningful baseline or a crippled one. The paper doesn’t show that SGDiff is competitive in the no-bbox regime beyond a single number.
- **Ablation (Sec. 5.2 / Table 4):** Holding iterations constant while varying data size changes the number of epochs/steps-per-sample; this tests “more diverse data at fixed compute” rather than sample efficiency. That’s a legitimate axis, but the interpretation “LAION‑Comp improves training efficiency” is not strictly supported without also holding *epochs* constant or reporting both.

### Conclusion (Sec. 6)
- Restates strong claims (“addressing the core problem of unstructured training data”) more strongly than the evidence warrants given the evaluation/identification issues above.

## Strengths

- Clear, high-impact direction: scaling structured (object/attribute/relation) conditioning to LAION-scale aesthetics data is a meaningful resource contribution if the annotations and benchmark are sound.
- Training multiple backbones (diffusion + flow matching) with a unified SG encoder and showing consistent improvements suggests the approach is not totally backbone-specific (Tables 2–3).
- The dataset statistics (object counts; relation vs non-spatial relation distribution) are useful and point toward a harder relational regime than VG (Sec. 3.2).

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Human evaluation of compositional correctness on CompSGen Bench outputs** (at least on a representative subset), specifically for relations and attribute binding. Without it, the paper’s main accuracy gains are not credible because the metrics are coupled to auto-annotations.  
2. **Out-of-domain generalization:** evaluate models trained on LAION‑Comp on an external compositional benchmark not derived from LAION‑Comp (or construct a held-out benchmark from a different source) to rule out in-domain distribution matching.  
3. **Same-images control:** on a fixed image set, compare training with (a) original captions, (b) GPT‑4o captions rewritten to be image-faithful, (c) SGs—so the improvement can be attributed to structure rather than “better textual faithfulness” or domain.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Metric validity analysis:** show that SG‑IoU/Relation‑IoU correlates with human judgments of relation correctness (not just object presence) and that correlation holds across methods (not only those trained on LAION‑Comp).  
2. **Annotation bias analysis:** quantify systematic biases in GPT‑4o SGs (e.g., relation frequency artifacts like “surrounded by”) and test whether models inherit/amplify them.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Failure taxonomy on relations:** examples where SG‑IoU improves but the human-perceived relation is wrong (or vice versa). This would reveal whether the benchmark is measuring true relational semantics or extractor/annotation artifacts.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. Release a **small, fully human-annotated “gold” subset** of LAION‑Comp test/benchmark scenes (with adjudication) to anchor evaluation and enable future metric calibration.

## Overall Assessment

The paper’s core idea—large-scale structured annotations for compositional generation—is promising, and the reported numbers are strong. However, the central experimental evidence is undermined by an evaluation loop that appears to rely on automatically produced SG “ground truth” and likely automated SG extraction for scoring, making it unclear whether gains reflect real compositional correctness or better alignment to the paper’s own annotation/extraction biases. Coupled with an in-domain benchmark sampled from the same LAION‑Comp distribution, the current submission does not convincingly establish the headline claims about compositional control or the causal claim that data (rather than architecture) is the fundamental bottleneck. In its current form, I do not think the paper should be accepted based on evidence presented; it needs an evaluation redesign anchored to independent human judgment and/or independently annotated ground truth.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Evidence that structural annotations are substantially more accurate/faithful than captions on the same images**: Table 1 shows LAION-Comp’s annotations outperform LAION captions on SG-IoU+ / Entity-IoU+ / Relation-IoU+ (0.422/0.810/0.749 vs 0.306/0.631/0.557) while also capturing more objects per sample (6.39 vs 5.33), supporting the paper’s data-centric claim that explicit structure better matches image content than unstructured prompts.
2. **Demonstrated *dataset* advantage over prior SG datasets (COCO/VG) in controlled, same-model comparisons**: In Table 2, the same SG2IM method trained on LAION-Comp consistently beats training on COCO or Visual Genome (e.g., SDXL-SG trained on LAION-Comp achieves 20.1 FID / 0.558 SG-IoU / 0.856 Rel-IoU vs 30.0/0.497/0.833 on COCO and 21.9/0.546/0.800 on VG), isolating the benefit to LAION-Comp rather than architecture.
3. **Strong benchmarked gains on complex compositions relative to state-of-the-art baselines**: On CompSGen Bench (Sec. 3.3), Table 3 shows the best compositional accuracy comes from the paper’s SG-conditioned models (e.g., FLUX-SG reaches 0.851 SG-IoU and 0.776 Entity-IoU), exceeding prior SG2IM baselines listed (SGDiff, SG-Adapter) and far above text-only baselines on SG-IoU (e.g., SDXL 0.226).
4. **Scale + quality of structural annotations is empirically supported (not just asserted)**: Sec. 3.1 reports partial human verification with very high accuracies (98.8% objects / 97.5% attributes / 95.7% relations), providing concrete evidence that the automated GPT-4o pipeline produces reliable SG labels at LAION-scale.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Benchmark design targets the claimed failure mode (multi-object relations) with a clear operational definition**: Sec. 3.3 constructs CompSGen Bench by filtering the LAION-Comp test split to samples with “over four relations,” yielding 20,838 complex-scene cases and evaluating with relation-aware metrics (SG-IoU, Entity-IoU, Relation-IoU), aligning evaluation with the paper’s compositionality focus.
2. **Ablations connect performance to dataset size/coverage rather than accidental training effects**: Table 4 shows monotonic improvements as LAION-Comp data proportion increases for SDXL-SG variants (e.g., FID 31.6→21.9 and SG-IoU 0.522→0.546 from 10%→100%), strengthening the causal link between more LAION-Comp supervision and better compositional generation.
3. **Non-spatial, interaction-centric relation coverage differentiates the dataset from prior SG corpora**: Sec. 3.2 quantifies LAION-Comp’s relation distribution as predominantly non-spatial (77.48%), contrasting with VG’s spatial skew (58.02% spatial), evidencing a meaningful “harder/more semantic” relational focus than common prior SG datasets.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Concrete, model-agnostic conditioning interface with explicit formulation**: Sec. 4 specifies an SG-embedding construction (Eq. 1) and training objective for flow-matching backbones (Eq. 2), making the conditioning mechanism explicit and transferable across backbones (diffusion and flow matching), which is reflected in Table 3 results spanning SD1.5/SDXL/SD3.5/FLUX variants.

## Summary

The paper contributes a large-scale, high-quality scene-graph annotated dataset (LAION-Comp) and a dedicated complex-composition benchmark (CompSGen Bench) to address compositional failures in text-to-image models via explicit structural conditioning. The single most important evidence that it delivers is the controlled, same-architecture improvement when training on LAION-Comp versus COCO/VG (Table 2), where SDXL-SG trained on LAION-Comp achieves markedly better compositional accuracy (e.g., 0.856 Rel-IoU) alongside strong image quality (20.1 FID), demonstrating that scaling reliable structural annotations materially improves compositional generation beyond prior SG datasets and methods.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper introduces **LAION-Comp**, a 540K-image dataset with **GPT-4o–produced scene-graph annotations** (objects/attributes/relations) plus partial human verification, and **CompSGen Bench**, a 20,838-sample benchmark (subset of the LAION-Comp test split with >4 relations) for evaluating complex compositional generation. It also trains several SG-conditioned baselines (diffusion and flow-matching backbones) using a CLIP-initialized + GNN-refined SG encoder and reports improved SG-based compositional metrics over text-only and prior SG2IM methods.

## Strengths
- **Large-scale structured resource + targeted benchmark**: LAION-Comp (540,005 SG-image pairs) and CompSGen Bench (20,838 complex samples) are clearly specified in Sec. 3.2–3.3 (lines 59–66).
- **Controlled “same model, different dataset” evidence that LAION-Comp helps**: Table 2 shows SDXL-SG trained on LAION-Comp improves both FID and SG metrics relative to training on COCO/VG (e.g., FID 20.1 vs 30.0/21.9; Rel-IoU 0.856 vs 0.833/0.800; lines 116–121).
- **Method is at least concretely formulated**: SG embedding construction and training objective are explicitly given (Eq. 1–2; lines 71–79), including a stabilizing learnable scale factor α.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **Evaluation is not independently grounded; core “compositional correctness” claims are primarily agreement with an automated SG pipeline.**  
  The paper states LAION-Comp SGs are generated with GPT-4o (line 40–41, 55) and CompSGen Bench evaluates “overlap … with the real annotations” via SG-IoU/Entity-IoU/Relation-IoU (lines 63–66). However, the main text does not describe any *independent* human evaluation of *model outputs* on CompSGen Bench, nor does it clearly describe how scene graphs are extracted from generated images for scoring (it references Sec. A.2/A.5). As written in the main paper, the headline improvements on Table 3 (lines 130–140) therefore establish “better match to the benchmark’s automated SG annotations/measurement,” but do not convincingly establish better human-perceived relational correctness.
- **Overstated causal narrative (“architecture doesn’t matter; data is the bottleneck”) is not supported by the presented design.**  
  The introduction explicitly claims the limitation is “not … architecture” and that prior architectural work “failed fundamentally” due to a data deficiency (lines 19–20, 28). Yet the paper simultaneously changes multiple factors: (i) dataset (LAION-Comp vs COCO/VG), (ii) conditioning interface (SG vs text), and (iii) a particular SG-encoder/integration pipeline (Sec. 4; lines 69–79). Table 2 helps, but it still doesn’t isolate “data vs architecture” in the strong causal sense claimed—especially because the benchmark itself is derived from LAION-Comp’s test split (lines 63–66), making dataset/benchmark coupling hard to disentangle from “general compositional ability.”

### Minor
- **CompSGen Bench is explicitly in-domain (subset of LAION-Comp test) and thus limited as a generalization claim.**  
  The benchmark is sampled from the LAION-Comp test set (lines 63–66), so the main results primarily measure in-distribution performance. The paper mentions evaluation on T2I-CompBench only via “details in Sec. A.6” (line 141–142), which is too de-emphasized in the main narrative given the broad “unlocking”/general paradigm framing.
- **The “SGs are more compact than text” claim conflicts with the paper’s own length statistics.**  
  Table 1 shows SG “length” 32.2 vs caption length 19.0 (lines 32–37), while the text still calls SG “compact” (line 20; reiterated around lines 61–62). This is not about style; it weakens an argument used to motivate SGs (the stronger claim is “more explicit/unambiguous,” which the paper also states).

### Trivial
None (parser artifacts and formatting issues are ignored by policy).

## Nice-to-Haves
- Add a small **human-annotated “gold” subset** for CompSGen Bench (or a human study on relation/attribute binding for a representative subset of generated outputs) and report correlation between SG-IoU/Rel-IoU and human judgments.
- Elevate the **out-of-domain** evaluation (e.g., T2I-CompBench) into the main paper with key numbers, given the benchmark’s in-domain construction.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“Benchmark uses the model’s own SG extractor as ground truth.”** The paper text we can see does *not* state that the *same* extractor is shared with the evaluated models; it only says the benchmark uses “real annotations” and computes overlaps (lines 63–66). The concern about automated GT is kept (Major), but the specific “model’s own extractor as GT” coupling is not verifiable from the provided main-text extract.
- **“CLIP score usage is unusual and needs justification.”** The paper defines CLIP as “similarity between generated and ground truth images” (line 65). This is somewhat nonstandard, but without the appendix details (Sec. A.2) it’s hard to judge whether it’s inappropriate; also it’s not central compared to the SG metrics.

## Novel Insights
The paper’s strongest empirical evidence (Table 2) is also its most conceptually ambiguous: because CompSGen Bench is drawn from LAION-Comp’s test split, improvements from training on LAION-Comp may reflect a tight coupling between (i) the dataset’s annotation conventions and (ii) the benchmark’s measurement protocol, rather than a general improvement in compositional semantics. This suggests the paper would be substantially stronger if it reframed its central claim as “high-scale structural annotation improves performance *on structurally evaluated, in-domain complex scenes*,” unless it can anchor the metrics to human perception and show robust transfer to external benchmarks in the main body.

## Suggestions
- Temper the introduction’s exclusivity: revise “not architecture” / “failed fundamentally” (lines 19–20, 28) to a claim the experiments actually identify (e.g., “data + structured supervision are a major bottleneck”).
- In the main paper, include: (1) a brief description of how SG-IoU/Rel-IoU are computed from generated images, and (2) a human-evaluation sanity check demonstrating the metrics track relation correctness.
- Promote out-of-domain results (currently relegated to Sec. A.6 per line 141–142) into the main results table or main-text discussion.

## Score and Decision
**Calibration note:** I was unable to run `calibration_search` due to a tool error (“not a valid model ID”), so I cannot provide the required anchored comparison to high/medium/low human-scored papers from `/home/wg25r/review_agent/human_reviews_2026`. This prevents proper score calibration per instructions.

Given the paper’s clear resource contribution and strong in-domain numbers, but with **major** concerns about **independent validity of the main evaluation** and **overclaimed causal conclusions**, my (uncalibrated) assessment is **borderline-to-negative** for ICLR as-is.

