### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **The “boundary-free” image-feature baseline is not actually boundary-free, and the paper’s central claimed mechanism is underspecified. (Structural)**  
   **Where:** Sec. 3.3 (and the claim repeated in the Abstract/Contributions), Fig. 7, Eq. (1).  
   **Why it breaks the core claim:** The paper argues image features “are not constrained by perception range” and “do not have out-of-scope issues unlike BEV features” (Sec. 3.3). But the proposed method *still* requires (i) the agent to project into one of the finite set of camera images, (ii) the agent to be within the camera’s effective FOV/visibility, and (iii) the relevant context to be present at sufficient pixel resolution. Far-away agents are exactly where camera projection and resolution are most brittle. Calling this “boundary-free” is conceptually wrong unless the benchmark explicitly defines “scope” as only the map-model’s BEV crop and then demonstrates that camera/FOV missingness is negligible—which it does not.  
   Additionally, Eq. (1) is a black box: it’s unclear what the query/key/value are, how multi-view fusion is done (they define \(T(i)\) as a single chosen view), how occlusions are handled, what happens when the projection lands outside the feature map, and what supervision (if any) encourages those retrieved features to encode map-like context rather than agent appearance. As written, the baseline is not a well-defined intervention on the claimed failure mode, so the paper’s headline “boundary-free” mitigation claim is not supported.

2) **The new split conflates two goals (removing map train/val overlap vs. matching map-error distributions) and may invalidate “generalization” conclusions; the benchmark design choice is not justified as the right scientific control. (Evidential)**  
   **Where:** Sec. 3.2, Fig. 3–4, Table 1, Sec. 4.1 (“generalization ability” claim).  
   **Why it matters:** The split is motivated by (a) removing spatial overlap to assess map-model generalization, and (b) eliminating the motion-model train/val gap by ensuring the map model has never seen either motion subset. But the proposed design *bakes in* a particular error distribution: motion training is *always* trained on maps generated out-of-distribution for the map model (relative to map-train), rather than training motion on “as-good-as-possible maps” and evaluating robustness to degraded maps. That is a legitimate benchmark choice, but it is not “eliminating a misconception” so much as choosing one operating point on the realism/robustness axis.  
   Table 1 compares several split settings but does not disentangle: is the gain from reduced spatial overlap, from worse maps at motion-train time (regularization), from different scene composition, or from simply changing which scenes are in motion train/val? Without reporting the *map-model accuracy distribution* on motion-train vs motion-val (even with a model-agnostic proxy) or at least showing that the map error statistics match across motion-train and motion-val, the “train-val gap eliminated” and “better evaluates generalization” claims are not established.

3) **Evaluation changes (non-ego only, moving-only, close/far) are sensible, but the paper overstates that prior metrics are “incorrect” and the new metrics are valid without demonstrating they measure the intended safety/interaction difficulty. (Methodological gap)**  
   **Where:** Sec. 3.3–3.4, Fig. 8, Table 6–7.  
   **Why it matters:** Excluding static agents and separating close/far is plausible, but the paper never checks whether the “moving >2m in 3s” rule actually selects the hard/interactive cases (e.g., merges, yields, intersections) rather than just higher-speed straight driving. Likewise, “close/far decided by whether within the perception range” ties difficulty to the map-model crop rather than to interaction relevance. This does not invalidate the benchmark, but it weakens the claim that the benchmark is “well-defined and insightful” rather than merely “different.” At minimum, the benchmark needs evidence that these strata correlate with distinct error modes or planning-critical scenarios.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Intro
- The intro frames three “misconceptions” as if they are objective errors in prior work. Two are really *design choices*: (i) whether to train the downstream on in-distribution vs out-of-distribution maps, and (ii) whether the benchmark’s target is ego planning vs scene forecasting. The paper should position these as benchmark goals/tradeoffs, not as prior “misuse,” unless it can show that the prior protocol’s stated goal was the same as OMMP-Bench’s.
- Claim that ego-only evaluation is “against the purpose of motion prediction” (Sec. 3.4) is too strong: ego forecasting can be a meaningful proxy in some stacks; the real criticism is that it fails to test the specific *range mismatch* failure mode the paper focuses on.

### Sec. 3.2 (Splits)
- Fig. 3 caption says proposed setting is “Lower” but later text says “as shown in Fig. 3 (Upper)”—likely a parser artifact, but the logical flow is confusing.
- Table 1: Split (4) (half/half) is interesting but underexplained; it seems like an attempt to avoid map-model training leakage into motion-model training while keeping same overall distribution, but the paper does not interpret it. Also, no variance / multiple seeds are shown; these deltas are not huge, so stability matters.

### Sec. 3.3 (Range mismatch + baseline)
- Table 2 is garbled; but the key claim is “extend range → map performance collapses.” The paper never shows that the collapse is due to geometry/range rather than insufficient capacity/training changes; nor does it consider multi-scale or curriculum approaches. That’s fine if the paper’s goal is benchmarking, but then it should avoid concluding “cannot meet requirements” as if it were a hard limitation.
- Table 3 compares GT maps at 30×60 vs 100×100 for HiVT and shows modest gains; this does not by itself establish that far agents are map-starved in the online setting. You need an analysis of how many agents fall outside map coverage and how performance degrades as a function of distance *conditional on map availability*.

### Sec. 3.4 (Metrics)
- The paper flips between “only evaluate non-ego agents” (earlier) and Fig. 8/Table 6 which still reports Ego plus other groups. That’s fine, but the benchmark definition should be crisp: what is the official primary score?
- MR definition is not specified (threshold, horizon). This affects interpretability.

### Sec. 3.5 (Map formulation)
- Table 5: Concluding “centerlines most helpful” from minADE alone is risky because different element sets change input density; the motion model may simply benefit from more polylines or better spatial coverage. The analysis needs normalization (e.g., fixed polyline budget) to support semantic conclusions.

### Sec. 4 (Experiments)
- Table 7: The “img” baseline improves far-agent metrics substantially, but because the baseline adds a new sensor-derived pathway, it is not isolating “map vs no-map” effects anymore; it becomes a *fusion* method. That’s a valid baseline, but it muddies the benchmark’s stated purpose (“study the impact of online maps”) unless the benchmark clearly treats “online map model features” as permissible general perception features and not map-specific outputs.
- No discussion of compute / latency: adding deformable attention over multi-view features per agent could be expensive; a benchmark proposal should at least report rough overhead.

## Strengths

- The paper identifies a real protocol pitfall in two-stage pipelines: training the downstream on “teacher-forced” upstream outputs (map predictions on the upstream’s training set) and evaluating on degraded upstream outputs (Sec. 3.2, Fig. 3). This is a genuine evaluation hazard.
- The moving-only and close/far stratification (Sec. 3.4, Table 6–7) is directionally the right response to nuScenes’ large fraction of easy/static trajectories and to the range mismatch the paper targets.
- The split sizes and concrete benchmark recipe (Sec. 4.1) make the proposal actionable.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Show that the proposed split actually equalizes upstream map quality between motion-train and motion-val.** Without this, “train-val gap eliminated” is an assertion, not a demonstrated property (Sec. 3.2, Table 1).  
2. **Ablate the “img” baseline to verify it addresses out-of-map context rather than adding generic capacity.** E.g., compare to adding the same parameter/attention budget but with random/proxy features, or restrict to only agents outside map range to see whether gains concentrate where claimed (Sec. 3.3, Table 7).  
3. **Quantify the true “out-of-scope” rate.** Report the fraction of evaluated agents that lie outside the map model range, and performance vs distance bins; otherwise the benchmark’s core motivation is not empirically grounded (Sec. 3.3–3.4).

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Define what “boundary-free” means operationally and analyze failure cases.** If agents are outside camera FOV / heavily occluded, the image-feature approach also fails; the paper needs to characterize that regime to justify the terminology (Sec. 3.3).  
2. **Disentangle semantic-type benefit from polyline density/coverage.** Centerlines may help because they cover more drivable space, not because they are intrinsically special (Sec. 3.5, Table 5).

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Qualitative examples where ego improves but non-ego worsens** under “unc”/“bev” methods (claimed in Sec. 4.2). Without cases, it’s hard to trust the interpretation that the metric change reveals a real weakness rather than noise.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Establish a single primary leaderboard metric and evaluation protocol.** Right now the benchmark reports multiple groups; it should clearly define which is the official score to optimize (Sec. 3.4, Table 7).

## Overall Assessment

The paper is a useful attempt to standardize evaluation for online-map-based motion prediction, and the split/metric critiques are largely directionally correct. However, the submission overclaims on its new “boundary-free” baseline (it is not boundary-free in any meaningful sensing sense, and the method is underspecified), and it does not provide the key evidence that OMMP-Bench’s new split actually achieves its stated statistical control (matching upstream error distributions across motion-train and motion-val). These gaps directly undermine the paper’s central framing that it has “corrected misconceptions” and produced a “well-defined” benchmark. I would not recommend acceptance in its current form.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Identifies and empirically demonstrates the two-stage “train–val gap” caused by default splits, and provides a concrete fix that measurably helps**: Fig. 3 contrasts the default protocol (map inferred on the map-model’s *training* set for motion-model training) with the proposed split/protocol, and Table 1 shows the new split (Setting 1) improves downstream motion prediction vs the default (Setting 3), e.g., minADE **0.6308 vs 0.6839** and MR **0.1558 vs 0.1732** (MapTRv2-CL + HiVT, evaluated on “all moving non-ego vehicles”).
2. **Corrects a prior evaluation blind spot (ego-only metrics) and replaces it with agent-centric, difficulty-sensitive metrics that reveal different behavior than ego-only reporting**: Sec. 3.4 and Fig. 8 define evaluating *moving non-ego* agents (and separating “close” vs “far” by map range), and Table 6 quantifies that static agents are nearly trivial (e.g., HiVT+MapTR “Static” minADE **0.002**) while far non-ego is substantially harder (HiVT+MapTR “Moving Non-Ego Far” minADE **0.6997**), justifying the new metric design over the prior ego-only protocol (Sec. 1, Sec. 3.4).
3. **Diagnoses the map-range mismatch as a real downstream failure mode and shows why “just predict longer-range maps” is not a viable remedy**: Sec. 3.3 ties limited map model range (e.g., 30×60m) to missing context for agents >100m away, and Table 2 shows map quality collapses when extending range (e.g., MapTRv2-CL **0.164 → 0.002** from 30×60m to 100×100m), while Table 3 shows long-range *GT* maps help motion prediction (HiVT minADE **0.6154 → 0.6003**), supporting the paper’s claim that the bottleneck is long-range *online* mapping rather than motion models.
4. **Introduces and validates a “boundary-free” image-feature baseline that improves far-agent prediction where online maps are missing**: Sec. 3.3 formalizes per-agent image feature aggregation via Deformable Attention (Eq. (1)) and motivates it as not constrained by the online map’s BEV range; Table 7 shows consistent gains for the proposed “img” variant, especially for far non-ego agents (e.g., MapTR+HiVT far minADE **0.6997 → 0.6318**; MapTRv2-CL+HiVT far minADE **0.6999 → 0.6274**), aligning with the stated goal of mitigating out-of-scope agents.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Provides a controlled analysis of which map element types matter for motion prediction, offering actionable guidance to online mapping output design**: Sec. 3.5 and Table 5 run an ablation over divider/boundary/ped-crossing/centerline, showing centerlines are particularly informative (centerline-only minADE **0.6631**, second best) and “all elements” is best (**0.6308**), which is more diagnostic than prior protocols that feed only one element type depending on availability (Sec. 3.5).
2. **Benchmarks multiple upstream/downstream combinations under the corrected protocol, enabling more reliable comparative conclusions than earlier ego-only / default-split evaluations**: Table 7 reports results across two map models (MapTR, MapTRv2-CL), two motion models (HiVT, DenseTNT), and multiple integration methods (base/unc/bev/img) with separate ego/close/far metrics—e.g., showing improvements that appear on ego do not necessarily transfer to non-ego close/far (Sec. 4.2 discussion grounded in Table 7).

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Clear protocol visualizations that make the benchmark changes reproducible and harder to misinterpret**: Fig. 3 lays out the four-step pipeline and highlights where the default protocol leaks training-set map predictions into motion training; Fig. 8 visually clarifies the agent selection change (ego-only vs moving agents), directly supporting correct adoption.

## Summary

The paper contributes OMMP-Bench, a corrected benchmark/protocol for online-map-based motion prediction that fixes key misconceptions (two-stage split-induced train–val gap, ego-only and static-heavy metrics, and map-range mismatch) and proposes a boundary-free image-feature baseline to handle out-of-range agents. The single most important evidence that it delivers on this contribution is Table 7 (supported by Fig. 3/8), which—under the new split and refined metrics—shows the proposed image-feature integration improves non-ego far-agent prediction substantially (e.g., MapTR+HiVT far minADE **0.6997 → 0.6318**; MapTRv2-CL+HiVT far minADE **0.6999 → 0.6274**), demonstrating both the necessity of the benchmark fixes and the effectiveness of the baseline under those fixes.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper proposes **OMMP-Bench**, a revised benchmark/protocol for **online-map-based motion prediction** on nuScenes. It argues that (i) the default two-stage protocol induces a train–val distribution gap due to using map predictions on the map model’s training split for motion training, (ii) ego-only and static-heavy metrics obscure failures on other agents, and (iii) map range is misaligned with motion prediction needs; it introduces new splits/metrics and an **image-feature (“img”) baseline** using deformable attention to mitigate “out-of-scope” agents.

## Strengths
- **Clearly identifies and demonstrates the two-stage split-induced train–val gap, and provides a concrete corrective split with measurable downstream gains** (Fig. 3 + Table 1: Setting 1 improves over Default Setting 3, e.g., minADE 0.6308 vs 0.6839; MR 0.1558 vs 0.1732).
- **Defines agent-centric metrics that expose easy/static cases and distance-stratified difficulty** (Sec. 3.4 + Table 6: “Static” minADE ≈ 0.002 vs far moving non-ego much worse, e.g., 0.6997 for HiVT+MapTR).
- **Empirically shows the range tension: extending map-model range degrades map quality, while longer-range GT maps help motion prediction** (Sec. 3.3 + Table 2/3: MapTRv2-CL map metric 0.164→0.002 when 30×60→100×100; HiVT with GT maps improves 0.6154→0.6003).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **The paper’s “boundary-free” baseline is overclaimed and underspecified relative to the mechanism it is used to justify.** The text claims image features “do not have out-of-scope issues unlike BEV features” (Sec. 3.3) and presents Eq. (1) as the core operation:  
  \[
  \hat A_i=\text{DeformAtt}(A_i,p_i,I_{T(i)})
  \]
  But the method description only states: project each agent to a **single chosen view** \(T(i)\) and apply deformable attention on that view’s feature map (Sec. 3.3). It does not specify (i) how \(T(i)\) is selected across cameras, (ii) what happens for agents projected outside the feature map / behind the camera / occluded, or (iii) how multi-view fusion is handled (if at all). This makes it hard to interpret the “img” gains in Table 7 as specifically addressing *map-range mismatch* rather than being an under-described additional perception pathway.
- **Key benchmark claims about the new split (“accuracy of online maps is similar”, “train–val gap eliminated”, “better evaluates generalization”) are asserted more than demonstrated.** The paper states: “we make sure the online mapping model has never encountered data from the two motion related subsets and thus the accuracy of online maps is similar. As a result, the motion prediction model adapts to the less precise maps, eliminating the train-validation gap” (Sec. 3.2; reiterated in Sec. 4.1). However, beyond downstream motion metrics in Table 1, the paper does not directly report or proxy-quantify **map quality distributions on motion-train vs motion-val** to verify the claimed “similar accuracy” control, nor does it isolate whether improvements stem from reduced overlap vs other distribution shifts induced by the new partition.

### Minor
- **The “moving agent” filter and close/far stratification are reasonable but not validated as selecting planning-critical interactions.** “Moving” is defined as “moves more than two meters within three seconds” (Sec. 3.4), and “close/far” is defined by whether within the map model’s perception range (Sec. 3.4). The paper shows the strata differ in difficulty (Table 6), but does not analyze whether these strata correspond to distinct interaction/safety-relevant regimes versus simply speed/distance effects.
- **Semantic conclusion about map element types is confounded by input density/coverage.** Sec. 3.5 concludes “centerlines are most helpful” based on Table 5 (minADE), but different element subsets change the number/coverage of polylines the model receives; without normalizing for a fixed polyline/point budget, it’s unclear whether the gain is semantic or just more/denser structure.
- **Official benchmark “primary metric” is not crisply specified.** The narrative says “only evaluate non-ego agents” (Sec. 1) but Fig. 8 describes evaluating “ego vehicle and other moving agents,” and Table 7 reports separate ego/close/far groups. A benchmark should clearly state what the main leaderboard target is.

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves
- Provide a small analysis of the **fraction of evaluated agents that are out-of-map-range** (by distance bins) and performance vs distance, to better ground the benchmark’s motivating failure mode (Sec. 3.3–3.4).
- Report rough **runtime/latency overhead** of the “img” deformable-attention integration since it changes the inference pathway.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
