### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **Diffusion “completion” supervision is internally inconsistent and likely encourages copying the diffusion output, not reconstructing the real scene (Structural).**  
   *Where:* Sec. 3.2.3, Eqs. (12)–(20).  
   The paper’s second-phase training uses “complete pseudo views” \(\hat I_p\) produced by an I2V diffusion model as supervision, via losses that directly penalize differences between \(I_p\) (rendered?) and \(\hat I_p\) (generated): \(L_{reg}=\|I_p-\hat I_p\|_1\odot \hat M_r\) and \(L_{str}=\text{LPIPS}(I_p,\hat I_p)\), then adds \(L_{GC}=L_{img}+\alpha(L_{reg}+L_{str})\) into the overall loss. This is conceptually backwards if \(\hat I_p\) is meant to be a *prior* rather than *target*: you are training the 3DGS to match the diffusion model’s hallucinated content in precisely the regions where the diffusion model is least constrained (unobserved regions). The “mask” is also defined to select pixels with *large* appearance gaps (Eq. 14), i.e., the most suspicious regions, and then forces the model to match \(\hat I_p\) there (Eq. 16). That directly undermines the claim “mitigates hallucination”: it operationally uses hallucination as supervision.  
   This is not a missing ablation; it’s a mismatch between the stated goal (hallucination attenuation) and the mathematical training signal as written.

2) **The evaluation does not establish that improvements come from better 3D reconstruction rather than 2D generative priors or evaluation leakage through pseudo-views (Evidential).**  
   *Where:* Sec. 4.1 Tables 1–3; Sec. 3.2 pseudo-view generation and second-phase loss.  
   The method introduces a powerful external generator (I2V diffusion + CLIP conditioning) that produces additional “training views.” But the paper only reports standard image metrics (PSNR/SSIM/LPIPS, plus “AVGE”) on held-out views, without any analysis that disentangles:  
   - whether the model is learning scene geometry consistent with input views, vs. being steered toward plausible-looking content that may be inconsistent but happens to score well;  
   - whether the diffusion prior introduces dataset-level bias (e.g., generating canonical textures/structures) that inflates LPIPS/SSIM while corrupting geometry;  
   - whether the pseudo-views overlap with test viewpoints or otherwise reduce the “unseen viewpoint” difficulty (the perturbed trajectory is designed to explore unobserved regions; without explicit reporting of pose distributions relative to test, this can inadvertently make the evaluation easier).  
   Given the central claim is *high-fidelity NVS* via *scene completion*, the current evidence is not enough to conclude the method improves *scene completion of the underlying scene* rather than producing better-looking but less faithful renderings.

3) **The point-cloud completion module is under-justified for per-scene few-shot SfM point clouds and may not be learnable in the claimed setting (Evidential).**  
   *Where:* Sec. 3.1.1–3.1.2; Sec. 4 (no training details for CPG/CPF).  
   CPG is presented as an end-to-end learned generator (DGCNN + Transformer + FoldingNet) producing complementary points. But the paper never states *how it is trained*: on what data distribution, with what supervision, whether it is pretrained on generic shapes/scenes, or trained per-scene (which would be ill-posed from few-shot). This matters because the claimed contribution is “generative point cloud completion-based Gaussian initialization” as a key novelty; without a credible training story, the module’s output quality and generalization are not established. The CPF filtering is simple, but it cannot salvage a poorly trained (or untrained) CPG; conversely, if CPG is heavily pretrained on domain-specific data, the gains might be mostly from external priors rather than the proposed method per se.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract + Introduction (Sec. 1)
- The framing “lack the human imagination” is motivationally fine, but the technical claim is “mitigates hallucination” while using generative outputs as supervision later; this tension shows up immediately in the method.  
- Claimed gains (“up to 2.40 dB …”) are plausible, but the paper does not clarify whether improvements are uniform or driven by a subset of scenes.

### Related Work (Sec. 2)
- The positioning versus ReconX/ViewCrafter/CAT3D is plausible, but the novelty claim “for the first time” (point cloud completion for Gaussian init) is risky: there is a large body of completion/upsampling for initialization in 3D representations; the paper needs very precise differentiation (what is new in *this* completion + filtering + attribute cloning pipeline for 3DGS few-shot).

### Method: GCGI (Sec. 3.1)
- The core missing piece is training/provenance of CPG (not a “clarification”: without it, the module is not scientifically grounded).  
- CPF thresholding: using \(\mu(P_0)\) defined as the mean pairwise distance over *all* points (Eq. 7) seems scale- and density-dependent and potentially unstable across scenes; it is not clear why this is the right normalization versus local densities or robust statistics.  
- Attribute cloning for new Gaussians (Sec. 3.1 end): copying non-position attributes from nearest \(P_0\) point could smear appearance and bias the optimization; the paper should justify why this doesn’t create artifacts or overly constrain new regions.

### Method: GCGO (Sec. 3.2)
- The pseudo-view diffusion conditioning is described vaguely: CLIP encodes training views to get \(F_c\), then used to condition an I2V model. This is a major component, yet the exact conditioning mechanism, which I2V model, and how camera control is enforced are not specified in the main text.  
- The “perturbed camera trajectory” is heuristic; the “see-saw effect” is plausible, but there is no principled argument that the chosen sinusoidal perturbation explores *true* unseen regions rather than simply generating harder views the diffusion model fills with priors.

### Experiments (Sec. 4)
- Metrics: “AVGE” is used heavily but not defined in the extracted text (presumably in appendix). Since it is used to argue hallucination/quality, its definition matters for claim validity.  
- Baselines: there is a mixture of NeRF, 3DGS, diffusion-guided approaches. Configuration fairness is not discussed (same #iterations, same compute, same pose sampling, same depth/SfM initialization quality). Given GenCoGS adds heavy components (diffusion + point completion), compute/latency and training-time comparisons are important for an ICLR acceptance bar in this area.  
- Variance/significance: no error bars, no per-scene spread; with few-shot NVS, results can be highly scene-dependent.

### Ablations (Sec. 4.3)
- The ablation suggests GCGO alone keeps LPIPS unchanged (Baseline 0.184 vs +GCGO 0.184) while boosting PSNR; that pattern is consistent with over-smoothing or metric tradeoffs, but the paper interprets it as better completion. Needs deeper analysis.  
- Table 5/6 are garbled by parsing, but even so, the ablation does not isolate the key risk: whether the diffusion pseudo-views introduce incorrect geometry that still looks good.

## Strengths

- Targets a real failure mode of sparse-view 3DGS pipelines: incomplete initialization and poor behavior in unobserved regions (Fig. 1 motivation is on-point).  
- The “generate-and-filter” idea for point completion is a reasonable attempt to control outliers rather than naïvely densifying.  
- Empirically, the reported gains on DTU and Shiny under 3-view are large enough to be interesting if the evaluation truly reflects faithful reconstruction.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Geometry/consistency evaluation** (e.g., depth error vs DTU ground truth, reprojection consistency, multi-view photometric consistency on *real* held-out views) because current metrics can improve while geometry worsens; this is central to “scene completion” claims.  
2. **Ablate the diffusion supervision target vs prior role**: compare (a) matching \(\hat I_p\) as a target (current), (b) using diffusion only to propose *regions*/*uncertainty* but supervising with real-view consistency, and (c) no \(\hat I_p\) pixel/feature matching—because the core claim is hallucination mitigation.  
3. **Report pose overlap / nearest-neighbor test-view distance** between pseudo-view camera poses and test poses, because otherwise “unseen viewpoint” evaluation may be inadvertently eased.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Failure cases where diffusion is wrong**: show whether the 3DGS converges to incorrect structures in unobserved regions; without this, “mitigation” is unsubstantiated.  
2. **CPG training/source**: what data, objective, and generalization regime; otherwise the “generative point completion” component is not scientifically interpretable.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Cross-view consistency checks**: render the same completed region from multiple nearby test poses and show whether content is stable (not just one-off pretty frames).  

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Compute and runtime accounting** (diffusion sampling cost, training time) because the method adds substantial overhead; without it, the practical value relative to 3DGS baselines is unclear.

## Overall Assessment

The paper’s direction is promising, and the reported numbers are strong, but as written the core mechanism for “hallucination attenuation” in GCGO appears conceptually inconsistent: it explicitly trains the 3D representation to match diffusion-generated pseudo-views in the regions where diffusion most likely hallucinates. Combined with insufficient evidence that improvements reflect faithful 3D scene completion (rather than better-looking priors or easier evaluation via pseudo-view pose coverage), the current submission does not convincingly support its central claims, and I would not recommend acceptance in its current form.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Addresses a specific failure mode of prior few-shot 3DGS pipelines with *two* complementary generative completion interventions (init + opt), and validates each one matters**: The method’s two-strategy design (GCGI for initialization in §3.1; GCGO for optimization in §3.2) is directly supported by the LLFF 3-view ablation (Table 4), where **+GCGI** improves the baseline (PSNR 20.79→21.45, SSIM 0.733→0.757, LPIPS 0.184→0.168, AVGE 0.096→0.087), **+GCGO** also improves (PSNR 20.79→21.65; AVGE 0.096→0.088), and **combined is best** (PSNR 22.13; LPIPS 0.164; AVGE 0.084).
2. **Demonstrates state-of-the-art quantitative gains over strong 3DGS and diffusion-guided baselines on multiple datasets and few-shot regimes**: On LLFF across 3/6/9 views (Table 1), GenCoGS achieves best overall metrics (e.g., 3-view PSNR **22.13** vs BinoGS 21.44; 6-view PSNR **25.61** vs BinoGS 24.87; 9-view PSNR **26.64** vs BinoGS 26.17) and best AVGE (3-view **0.084** vs 0.097/0.101 for CAT3D/BinoGS). On DTU 3-view (Table 2), it posts a large jump (PSNR **23.11** vs CAT3D 22.02 / MuRF 21.31; LPIPS **0.082** vs 0.121/0.127) and especially AVGE (**0.049**, markedly lower than prior work). On Shiny 3-view (Table 3), it improves substantially (PSNR **21.10** vs FSGS 19.63; SSIM **0.692** vs 0.612; LPIPS **0.202** vs 0.327).
3. **Introduces a concrete, optimization-free outlier suppression mechanism for generative point completion that is tailored to few-shot instability**: The CPF module (§3.1.2) explicitly avoids adding learnable structure (motivated in §3.1.2) and instead prunes hallucinated points by a kd-tree neighbor distance test against SfM points (Eq. 5–7), yielding a “generate-and-filter” completion pipeline (Figure 2) with visual evidence that filtering improves point quality (Figure 3 shows P0 vs Pc vs Pf).
4. **Directly tackles diffusion hallucination in pseudo-view supervision with an explicit mask-and-regularize training signal, not just “use diffusion prior”**: The generative consistency loss in §3.2.3 defines a pixelwise distortion detector via color-gap statistics (Eq. 12–15), then applies a masked L1 regularizer (Eq. 16) plus a perceptual/LPIPS structural term (Eq. 17), forming the overall GC loss (Eq. 18) and final training schedule (Eq. 20). This is differentiated from prior diffusion-guided NVS that primarily uses diffusion outputs as extra views without an explicit per-pixel hallucination attenuation mechanism.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Pseudo-view pose sampling is engineered to cover unobserved regions and is empirically justified**: The perturbed camera trajectory (§3.2.1, Eq. 11) is evaluated in Table 5, where “Camera Trajectory” sampling plus the GC loss produces the best numbers (PSNR **22.13**, SSIM **0.762**, LPIPS **0.164**) relative to random sampling and unperturbed trajectory variants.
2. **Robustness of the completion-based initialization is tested under degraded SfM point availability**: The “1/4 points” setting in the CPG/CPF ablation table (Table 6 as parsed) shows consistent improvements when enabling CPG then CPF (e.g., PSNR 21.24 → 21.61 → 21.78), supporting the claim in §4.3 that GCGI generalizes when the initial point cloud is poorer.
3. **Qualitative evidence targets exactly the claimed artifacts (hollows/floating) and compares against both 3DGS-prior methods and diffusion pipelines**: Figures 5–6 (and the discussion in §4.2) explicitly contrast GenCoGS with DNGaussian/BinoGS (limited completion) and ViewCrafter (hallucination), and highlight corrected hollow/floating regions after applying GCGO.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Reproducibility-critical hyperparameters and training schedule are explicitly specified**: §4 lists key settings (5000 steps, densification cadence, switching to GCGO after m=4000, kd-tree k=3, thresholds δ1/δ2/δ3, perturbation A/f, and loss weights α/β), enabling a faithful reimplementation without relying solely on the appendix.

## Summary

The paper proposes GenCoGS, a unified few-shot 3D Gaussian Splatting approach that improves scene completion by (i) completing and filtering the SfM point cloud for better Gaussian initialization (§3.1) and (ii) generating diffusion-completed pseudo views with a hallucination-aware consistency loss for optimization (§3.2). The strongest evidence that it delivers is the consistent, cross-dataset quantitative superiority—especially DTU 3-view (Table 2: PSNR 23.11, SSIM 0.910, LPIPS 0.082, AVGE 0.049)—together with ablations (Table 4) showing both proposed strategies independently help and jointly yield the best performance.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
The paper proposes **GenCoGS**, a few-shot novel view synthesis method built on **3D Gaussian Splatting** that aims to improve *scene completion* under sparse inputs via (i) **generative point-cloud completion + filtering** for better Gaussian initialization (Sec. 3.1) and (ii) **diffusion-completed pseudo-views** plus a **generative consistency loss** during optimization (Sec. 3.2). It reports sizable gains on LLFF/DTU/Shiny in 3/6/9-view regimes (Tables 1–3).

## Strengths
- **Consistent quantitative improvements across datasets / view counts**: e.g., LLFF 3-view PSNR 22.13 vs BinoGS 21.44 and CAT3D 21.58 (Table 1, lines ~184–188), DTU 3-view PSNR 23.11 vs CAT3D 22.02 and MuRF 21.31 (Table 2, lines ~189–194).
- **Clear decomposition into two modules with an explicit mechanism for outlier pruning in point completion**: CPF uses kd-tree nearest-neighbor distances to prune generated points (Eqs. 5–8, lines ~76–101), which is a concrete attempt to control completion hallucinations in initialization.

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **The “hallucination attenuation” objective in GCGO is not convincingly aligned with the actual supervision signal**. In Sec. 3.2.3, the method defines a mask that activates where the diffusion-completed pseudo-view differs *most* from the initial pseudo-view (Eqs. 12–15; mask is 1 when ΔC(u,v) > T(u,v), lines ~142–155), then directly **penalizes** the rendered pseudo-view to **match the diffusion output** on those masked pixels via \( \mathcal{L}_{reg}=\|I_p-\hat I_p\|_1\odot \hat M_r \) (Eq. 16, lines ~157–160) and LPIPS between \(I_p\) and \(\hat I_p\) (Eq. 17, lines ~161–162). As written, this is training the 3D representation to imitate the diffusion completion precisely in regions flagged as “distorted,” which undercuts the paper’s claim that this loss *mitigates* diffusion hallucination rather than propagating it into the 3DGS.
- **Key scientific ambiguity: what is the training/provenance of the Complementary Point Generation (CPG) module?** Sec. 3.1.1 presents CPG as an “end-to-end” learned generator (DGCNN + Transformer + FoldingNet; Eqs. 1–4, lines ~41–67) but the extracted main text does not specify whether CPG is pretrained (and on what), trained jointly, or trained per-scene. Given few-shot SfM point clouds are extremely limited, this missing training story materially affects interpretability of the claimed contribution and whether gains come from a strong external shape prior versus the proposed pipeline.

### Minor
- **Evaluation evidence is heavily image-metric-based while the core claim is 3D *scene completion***. The paper emphasizes PSNR/SSIM/LPIPS and “AVGE” (Tables 1–2), but in the extracted text the definition of AVGE and any geometry/consistency measurement is deferred to the appendix (line ~200). Since the method introduces a powerful diffusion generator to create pseudo supervision, additional evidence targeting *multi-view/3D consistency* would better support the “completion” and “less floating artifacts” claims.
- **CPF thresholding may be scale/density sensitive**: the filter uses a global mean pairwise distance \(\mu(P_0)\) (Eq. 7, lines ~91–95) as normalization; this can vary strongly with scene scale and point density, and the paper does not justify robustness or offer sensitivity analysis.
- **Pseudo-view pose sampling could inadvertently reduce “unseen view” difficulty without being reported**: the perturbed trajectory is specified (Eq. 11, lines ~124–131), but the paper does not report how pseudo-view pose distribution relates to test views (e.g., nearest-neighbor pose distance), making it hard to rule out that pseudo-views cover test-like viewpoints.

### Trivial
None (parser/formatting artifacts ignored per instructions).

## Nice-to-Haves
- Provide explicit **runtime/compute accounting** for diffusion pseudo-view generation and overall training cost vs baselines, since the method adds substantial components beyond standard 3DGS optimization.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“AVGE is not defined”**: the main text explicitly says evaluation metric details are in the appendix (line ~200), and appendix content is known to be stripped by the parser; so this cannot be treated as a valid weakness.
- **“Exact I2V model / conditioning mechanism not specified in main text”**: the paper references details in “Preliminary in Appendix” for the diffusion formulation (lines ~120–122). Given parser stripping, lack of appendix detail here is not a reliable criticism.

## Novel Insights
The strongest acceptance-risk is not the presence of diffusion guidance per se, but that **the paper’s own “distortion detector” mask selects large diffusion-vs-initial discrepancies and then enforces *matching the diffusion output* there**, which is logically closer to “learn the diffusion completion” than “attenuate diffusion hallucination.” Unless the authors can articulate why \(\hat I_p\) is a higher-fidelity target in those masked regions (and empirically verify improved cross-view consistency), this mismatch threatens the central narrative more than typical missing-ablations critiques.

## Suggestions
- Reframe and/or modify GCGO so the diffusion model acts as a **prior** (e.g., regularize uncertainty, provide proposals) rather than a **pixel/feature target** in high-discrepancy regions; at minimum, add experiments showing improved **multi-view consistency/geometry** (not just 2D metrics) and analyze cases where diffusion completion is wrong.
- Clearly specify **CPG training**: dataset, supervision/objective, pretraining vs joint training, and what is fixed at test time; add an ablation separating “pretrained completion prior” effects from the rest of the pipeline.
- Report pseudo-view pose statistics relative to test views (e.g., nearest-neighbor pose distance) to rule out evaluation easiness due to pose overlap.

## Score and Decision
