### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **Planning evaluation is too underpowered and confounded to support the headline “up to 35% higher success” claim** (**Evidential**)  
   - **Where:** Sec. 4.2, Fig. 8 (described), claims in Abstract/Intro/Conclusion.  
   - **Why it matters:** The main downstream claim is that tactile grounding “translates to planning” with large gains. But the real-robot evaluation is **5 trials per task** from “distinct initial conditions,” open-loop, with CEM (stochastic) and no reporting of variance across seeds, confidence intervals, or significance. With n=5, a 35% absolute difference is the difference of **2 successes out of 5**—not robust.  
   - **Confounds not controlled:** VT-WM and V-WM differ not only by modality but potentially by **effective information budget** and **training signal** (extra tactile targets and tokens; potentially more parameters/compute; different context windowing). Without careful matching and repeated-seed analysis, the success-rate deltas are not reliable evidence for the size (or even existence) of the planning advantage.

2) **The “object permanence” and “causal compliance” metrics do not clearly measure what the paper claims under the given protocol** (**Structural**)  
   - **Where:** Sec. 4.1 (“Object Permanence,” “Causal Compliance”), Figs. 4 and 6, use of CoTracker + normalized Fréchet distance.  
   - **Why it breaks core claims:** The paper equates lower keypoint-trajectory Fréchet distance with (i) object permanence under occlusion and (ii) compliance with laws of motion for passive objects. But the described metric is fundamentally a **2D image-plane tracking proxy**, and it is not established that it disambiguates:
     - *Permanence* vs. “reappearance somewhere that yields a similar 2D track,” or tracker failures under occlusion. If the model produces visually plausible texture patches that allow CoTracker to latch on, the metric can improve without representing persistent object state.  
     - *Causal compliance* vs. camera/lighting artifacts, model-induced background motion, or tracker drift. The “objects not subject to any external force” assumption is also shaky in contact-rich scenes (table vibrations, incidental contacts, airflow/cloth dynamics), yet the protocol for declaring “no external force” is not specified.  
   - **Net:** These metrics can indicate rollout visual consistency, but they do **not** cleanly validate the physics-grounding claims being made (Newton’s laws, permanence as a latent belief across occlusion).

3) **No isolation of the claimed source of gains (tactile grounding) from alternative explanations** (**Evidential**)  
   - **Where:** Sec. 3.2 (architecture/training), Sec. 4 (all comparisons are VT-WM vs V-WM only).  
   - **Why it matters:** The paper attributes improvements to “contact grounding via tactile input,” but the experiments do not separate at least three distinct factors:
     - **Training-time multitask auxiliary supervision** (predicting tactile latents) vs **test-time tactile context** (conditioning on tactile at k) vs **model capacity/token count** changes.  
     - Whether gains come from *tactile at inference* or simply from *regularization / better representation learning* during training.  
   - **Missing discriminative ablations (examples):** VT-WM trained with tactile targets but **no tactile input at test**; VT-WM with tactile input **randomized/dropped** at test; parameter-matched V-WM with extra tokens; or a vision-only model trained with an auxiliary “contact prediction” head. Without these, the causal claim “touch grounding is responsible” is not established.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction
- The abstract quantifies “33% object permanence” and “29% compliance with laws of motion,” but these are derived from the CoTracker/Fréchet proxy (Sec. 4.1). As written, the language overcommits to **physical-law compliance**, which the evaluation does not directly measure.
- “First multi-task visuo-tactile world model” is plausible, but the paper should be careful: the novelty appears to be **(multi-task) latent WM + tactile embeddings + planning**, not introducing a new tactile encoder or a new WM training paradigm.

### Related Work
- Generally coherent positioning (foundation encoders for tactile; action-conditioned WMs). No major issues.

### Sec. 3 (Method)
- Architecture description is understandable at a high level, but several details are critical for interpreting results and are currently underspecified in the main text:
  - Whether VT-WM and V-WM are **parameter-matched**, trained for equal steps, with equal token counts/compute.  
  - How tactile tokens are structured (per-fingertip, per-frame), and whether tactile is fused at all timesteps or only in context.  
  - The loss mixes teacher forcing and sampled rollouts, but it is unclear whether **sampling is done identically** for both V-WM and VT-WM and whether the sampling horizon H is tuned per model (which can materially affect long-horizon quality).
- Planning: cost is ℓ2 distance in **visual latent space** to a goal image latent (Sec. 3.2.3). This is a weak objective for contact-rich manipulation (can be satisfied by visually similar but physically wrong states). The paper’s claim is that tactile improves model fidelity enough that this weak cost works better—but that’s plausible; still, it makes the planning evaluation even more sensitive to model/metric quirks.

### Sec. 4.1 (Contact Perception)
- Using successful real trajectories and evaluating rollouts under the same actions is a reasonable diagnostic, but:
  - The paper relies heavily on CoTracker + Fréchet distances; without validating that the tracker works reliably under the exact occlusions of interest (and that failures are comparable across models), the metric can be misleading.  
  - Reporting t-tests “across tasks” is not well-defined from the text. If the unit of analysis is “task,” n=5 is far too small; if it is trajectories within tasks, that needs to be specified, including multiple-comparisons handling and independence assumptions.
- Notably, “scribble with marker” shows degradation for causal compliance; this is important and should be discussed as a limitation rather than averaged away.

### Sec. 4.2 (Zero-shot Planning)
- The evaluation is the most important, and it is not convincing as currently presented due to low n and missing variance/seed control (Critical Issue #1).
- The open-loop execution setting makes outcomes highly sensitive to minor modeling errors and robot stochasticity; with only 5 trials/task, you cannot distinguish systematic improvement from luck or from slightly different initializations.

### Sec. 4.3 (Data Efficiency)
- The comparison is not clean:
  - VT-WM uses **planning** (CEM) while BC is **closed-loop** ACT (Sec. 4.3). These have very different failure modes and sample-efficiency profiles, and it is not obvious which is advantaged by open-loop vs closed-loop under resets and perception noise.  
  - Both use tactile+RGB as inputs (BC explicitly; VT-WM implicitly via context), but VT-WM also has a strong multi-task prior and an optimization-based controller; BC is a single policy trained on 20 demos. This is not an apples-to-apples test of “data efficiency” so much as “planning with a pretrained model vs learning a policy from scratch.”

## Strengths
- Clear motivation: tactile resolves genuine ambiguities for contact-rich manipulation, especially under occlusion.  
- Sensible high-level design: leverage strong pretrained encoders (Cosmos, Sparsh-X) and learn a multimodal action-conditioned latent dynamics predictor.  
- The qualitative failure modes highlighted (e.g., wiping above cloth but cloth moving in imagination) are compelling and align with known weaknesses of vision-only rollouts.

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Ablations that isolate tactile’s role**: (i) tactile at train only vs train+test; (ii) test-time tactile dropout/randomization; (iii) parameter/compute-matched V-WM with equal token budget. Without these, the causal attribution to tactile grounding is not supported.  
2. **Planning robustness with proper statistics**: ≥30 trials per task (or enough to get tight CIs), multiple CEM seeds per trial, report CI/significance. Otherwise “up to 35%” is not credible.  
3. **Metric validation for Sec. 4.1**: show CoTracker reliability under occlusion (e.g., manual spot checks; agreement with annotated keypoints; or a tracker-free metric such as object detector/segmentation consistency), and define “passive objects” rigorously for causal compliance.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **When does tactile help / hurt?** The degradation on “scribble with marker” suggests negative transfer or modality conflicts; the paper should analyze conditions where tactile misleads (surface compliance? intermittent contact? tool use?).  
2. **Failure analysis for planning gains**: categorize failures (loss of contact, premature release, wrong approach vector, etc.) and connect them to predicted tactile/vision latents.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. Show paired **predicted tactile latents/images** aligned with visual rollouts for key successes/failures (they mention appendix figs, but this should be central because “contact grounding” is the thesis).

### Obvious Next Steps
1. Incorporate **tactile into the planning objective** (even a simple contact/no-contact or slip penalty) to test whether tactile helps only via model learning or also as an explicit goal/constraint.

## Overall Assessment
The paper’s core idea—multitask visuo-tactile latent world models for contact-rich manipulation—is timely and potentially valuable, and the qualitative examples are suggestive. However, the central empirical claims are not currently supported at the level the paper asserts: the main physics metrics are not well-aligned with the claimed constructs (object permanence / Newtonian compliance), the planning results are statistically underpowered and lack robustness controls, and there are no ablations that isolate tactile grounding from confounds like added supervision/capacity. In its current form, I would not recommend acceptance because the evidence does not yet justify the headline conclusions.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Quantified improvement on *contact-specific* world-model consistency metrics (not just video quality)**: The paper evaluates VT-WM vs. V-WM using World Consistency Score components focused on contact reasoning—object permanence and causal compliance—implemented via CoTracker trajectories and *normalized Fréchet distance* (Sec. 4.1). VT-WM shows an average **≈33% reduction** for object permanence (Fig. 4; described with per-task significance tests in Sec. 4.1) and **≈29% average reduction** in hallucinated motion for causal compliance (Sec. 4.1; Fig. 6).

2. **Evidence that tactile grounding reduces specific failure modes of visual WMs under contact ambiguity/occlusion**: The paper provides a concrete counterexample where vision-only rollouts deform/move a cloth despite the robot wiping above it without contact (Sec. 4.2; Fig. 7), while VT-WM produces rollouts with “fewer artifacts and less variation,” directly matching the claimed advantage that tactile context disambiguates contact vs. no-contact.

3. **Demonstrated translation from better imagination to better *real-robot* planning success in contact-rich multi-step tasks**: Zero-shot CEM planning uses a purely vision goal cost, so tactile helps only through improved dynamics (Sec. 3.2.3), and the real-robot results show the largest gains exactly where contact should matter: **+35% on reach&push**, **+31% wipe cloth**, plus improvements on push fruits and stack cubes (Sec. 4.2; Fig. 8-left). This is stronger evidence than merely reporting rollout metrics because it validates the intended downstream use (planning).

4. **A multi-task visuo-tactile WM that fuses strong pretrained foundation encoders for both modalities**: VT-WM uses Cosmos visual latents and Sparsh-X tactile latents (Sec. 3.2.2), then learns a joint action-conditioned latent dynamics transformer with explicit design for multi-modal spatiotemporal modeling (Sec. 3.2.1; Fig. 3). Relative to prior tactile-dynamics work cited as task-specific (Sec. 2), this multi-task fusion with foundation encoders is a meaningful step toward general-purpose multimodal WMs.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Training procedure explicitly targets rollout stability (distribution shift) rather than only one-step prediction**: The objective combines teacher forcing (Eq. 1) with an autoregressive “sampling loss” over H steps (Eq. 2), following the long-horizon coherence motivation in Assran et al. (2025) (Sec. 3.2.2). This directly supports the paper’s emphasis on autoregressive rollout quality in Sec. 4.1.

2. **Planning setup isolates the benefit of touch to model fidelity, not to goal specification**: The planner’s cost is defined only in *visual latent* space to a goal image (Sec. 3.2.3), while tactile is used only in the initial context and dynamics. This makes the improved real-robot success (Sec. 4.2; Fig. 8-left) more convincingly attributable to better contact modeling rather than “cheating” with tactile goal signals.

3. **Evidence of data efficiency vs. a strong task-specific imitation baseline under limited demos**: On “place plate in dish rack,” fine-tuned VT-WM planning achieves **77%** vs **22%** for ACT behavioral cloning using the same 20 demonstrations (Sec. 4.3; Fig. 8-right). The comparison is particularly relevant because BC is closed-loop with RGB+tactile at inference, yet VT-WM still outperforms, supporting the claim that multi-task contact priors transfer.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Clear architectural schematic for multimodal/action fusion**: Fig. 3 specifies the vision/tactile latent streams, concatenation, transformer predictor, and modality-specific heads, making the approach reproducible at a system-design level even with abbreviated hyperparameter details.

## Summary

The paper introduces a multi-task visuo-tactile latent world model for contact-rich manipulation and shows that adding tactile context measurably improves physical coherence and downstream planning. The strongest evidence it delivers on this is the combination of (i) trajectory-based object permanence and causal compliance improvements using CoTracker and normalized Fréchet distance (Sec. 4.1; Figs. 4 and 6) and (ii) corresponding **real-robot zero-shot planning** gains that peak on multi-step contact tasks (Sec. 4.2; Fig. 8-left), despite using a purely visual goal cost (Sec. 3.2.3).

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper proposes a multi-task visuo-tactile latent world model (VT-WM) that fuses pretrained vision (Cosmos) and tactile (Sparsh-X) embeddings to improve contact-grounded rollouts for manipulation, and uses the model for open-loop CEM planning on a real robot. It reports improvements over a vision-only world model (V-WM) on proxy “world consistency” metrics (object permanence, causal compliance) and on a small set of real-robot planning tasks, plus a limited-demo adaptation comparison to behavioral cloning.

## Strengths
- **Clear multimodal WM formulation and planning use-case**: The model fuses vision/tactile latents and predicts both next-step latents, and is directly used inside CEM with a purely visual goal cost (Sec. 3.2.1–3.2.3, esp. “We do not provide the tactile modality as a goal signal…” in Sec. 3.2.3).
- **Empirical signal that tactile changes failure modes in contact ambiguity**: The “wipe above cloth” qualitative example contrasts V-WM hallucinated cloth motion vs VT-WM reduced artifacts under identical action conditioning (Sec. 4.2, Fig. 7 discussion).
- **Quantitative improvements on their chosen rollout-consistency proxies**: They report normalized Fréchet-distance reductions using CoTracker trajectories for “object permanence” and “causal compliance,” including task-wise paired t-tests and (for Fig. 4) a stated 95% CI (Sec. 4.1, Fig. 4 and causal-compliance paragraph around Fig. 6).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **Planning headline (“up to 35% higher success”) is supported by an underpowered evaluation**: Success rates are “averaged over five trials per task” (Sec. 4.2: “Fig. 8(left) reports success rates, averaged over five trials per task…”). With n=5, a 35% absolute gain corresponds to only ~2 additional successes, and the paper reports no uncertainty (CIs), statistical test, or sensitivity to planner stochasticity; this makes the magnitude—and possibly even the existence—of the claimed planning advantage hard to trust.
- **The paper’s “object permanence” / “laws of motion” claims overreach relative to the metric**: Both “Object Permanence” and “Causal Compliance” are operationalized as *2D keypoint-trajectory* similarity from CoTracker plus normalized Fréchet distance (Sec. 4.1). That can indicate video/rollout consistency, but it is not a direct measurement of latent belief across occlusion or Newtonian physical validity; moreover, “objects…not subject to any external force” is asserted but the protocol for selecting/validating such “passive objects” is not specified (Sec. 4.1 causal-compliance paragraph). The abstract’s phrasing “29% better compliance with the laws of motion” is therefore stronger than what is directly validated.
- **Causal attribution to “tactile grounding” is not isolated by ablations**: The main comparisons are VT-WM vs V-WM (Sec. 4.1–4.2), but VT-WM changes multiple factors at once (additional tactile input tokens and an additional prediction target/loss term for tactile in Eq. (1)–(2)). The paper does not include ablations to separate (i) train-time auxiliary tactile prediction vs (ii) test-time tactile conditioning vs (iii) increased token/information budget. Without these, the central claim that *tactile grounding* is the driver (rather than extra supervision/capacity) remains suggestive rather than established.

### Minor
- **Statistical reporting for Sec. 4.1 is unclear/inconsistent**: The paper says it runs “paired t-tests across tasks” (Sec. 4.1), but does not clearly define the unit of analysis (per-task aggregate? per-trajectory?) or justify assumptions (independence, multiple comparisons). This matters because the task count is only five, and the reported very small p-values would be surprising if n truly equals the number of tasks.
- **“Data efficiency” comparison mixes substantially different control paradigms**: In Sec. 4.3, VT-WM uses open-loop CEM planning while ACT behavioral cloning is closed-loop and both are trained/used differently (“continue training VT-WM” on augmented dataset vs training a policy from 20 demos). The result is interesting as “pretrained WM planning vs policy learning from scratch,” but it is not a clean test of data efficiency in a controlled setting.

### Trivial
None (style/formatting issues intentionally ignored).

## Nice-to-Haves
- Add a tracker-free or partially annotated validation for the Sec. 4.1 proxies (e.g., manual occlusion/permanence checks on a subset, or an object mask/pose consistency metric) to better align with the “permanence/physics” language.
- Incorporate tactile into the planning objective (even a simple contact/no-contact or slip penalty) to test whether tactile helps beyond improving the learned dynamics.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“Planning confounded by parameter/compute mismatch”**: While it is plausible, the main text does not state whether VT-WM and V-WM are parameter-matched, so this cannot be asserted as a factual flaw; it remains an *unverified concern* unless appendix details confirm mismatch.

## Novel Insights
A key tension in the paper is that its strongest downstream demonstration (real-robot planning) uses a deliberately weak/purely-visual cost (Sec. 3.2.3), which makes the planning result highly sensitive to (i) small improvements in rollout visual plausibility and (ii) stochasticity from CEM and open-loop execution; with only five trials per task, this sensitivity can easily inflate “up to X%” claims. Tightening the statistical protocol and adding ablations would likely do more for credibility than adding additional tasks.

## Suggestions
- Report uncertainty for planning: increase trials per task (or at least provide binomial CIs), run multiple CEM seeds per trial, and report per-task confidence intervals and/or an aggregated statistical test.
- Add ablations to isolate tactile’s role: (1) tactile targets during training but no tactile context at test; (2) VT-WM with test-time tactile dropout/randomization; (3) token/parameter-matched vision-only variant with equivalent supervision budget.
- Rephrase claims around “laws of motion” and “object permanence” to align with what CoTracker+Fréchet actually measures, or strengthen the evaluation to directly test the claimed constructs.

## Score and Decision
**Calibration note:** I was unable to run `calibration_search` due to a tool error (“not a valid model ID”), so I cannot provide the required high/medium/low human-score anchors. Given the rubric requirement, this is a blocking issue for a properly calibrated numerical score.

Given the verified issues above—especially the severely underpowered planning evaluation supporting the headline claim, plus missing ablations for causal attribution—my leaning is **Reject** as-is, despite a timely idea and promising qualitative evidence.

