### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **Structural — Evaluation protocol conflates “strategy quality” with post-hoc selection on the test set (invalidates headline ASR gains).**  
   The core effectiveness metric is “average ASR of the top 100 strategies with the highest ASR on \(T_{tst}\)” (Sec. 3.1, Eq. 6). This is effectively *test-set model selection*: you rank strategies using the evaluation set and then report performance on that same set. That procedure will systematically inflate AUTO-RT (and any method that generates more candidates or higher-variance candidates), and it breaks the central claim that AUTO-RT “improves success rates by up to 16.63%.” To support that claim, strategy selection must be done using training/validation intents (or a separate holdout) and only then evaluated once on \(T_{tst}\).

2) **Evidential (borderline structural) — “Defense Generalization Diversity (DeD)” is not a well-defined, controlled measure, so diversity/coverage claims are not established.**  
   DeD is defined as: “constructing defenses based on the successful attacks, and evaluating the ASR of second-round attacks on the defended model” (Sec. 3.1). But the paper does not specify: (i) what the “defense” is (fine-tuning? refusal training? filtering? prompt patching?), (ii) whether the defense budget is fixed across methods, (iii) whether defenses are trained on the same number of successful attacks per method (critical, since AUTO-RT succeeds more, it could induce a *stronger* defense and look worse—or conversely a *weaker* defense if attacks are more diverse but fewer are used), (iv) whether the defender uses the same base model and hyperparameters. Without a precise, equal-budget defender, DeD cannot be interpreted as “diversity” or “continued discovery,” and the paper’s diversity/coverage conclusions (Abstract; Sec. 3.2; Table 1 right; Sec. 3.3.1) are not currently supported.

3) **Evidential — Reward shaping via a “downgraded model” risks optimizing the wrong objective; FIR is heuristic and not shown to preserve target-model jailbreak optimality.**  
   Progressive Reward Tracking defines a shaped reward \(R_s\in\{0,1,2\}\) based on success on TM′ and TM (Sec. 2.3.3, Eq. 4–5). The paper explicitly notes this is *not* potential-based shaping, so optimal policies can change. The justification (“most cases with \(R_{TM’}=0\) also yield \(R_{TM}=0\)”) is not quantified, and FIR-based downgrade selection (Sec. 2.3.3; Fig. 4 description) is an ad hoc elbow criterion. As a result, the central methodological claim—PRT “preserv[es] attack effectiveness” while improving exploration—remains unproven. At minimum, the paper needs evidence that optimizing \(R_s\) does not systematically bias away from the best TM jailbreak strategies (e.g., counterexamples where TM′ success is anti-correlated with TM success, or where FIR-selected TM′ changes the ranking of strategies on TM).

4) **Methodological gap — Baselines are incomplete for the paper’s stated target (automated jailbreak strategy exploration), and several comparisons are not “like-for-like.”**  
   The paper positions itself against template-based automated systems (AutoDAN, Rainbow-Teaming, PAIR, etc.) but the main table (Table 1) compares only FS/IL/RL and DA; the “human template” comparisons are aggregated in Table 3 without per-model breakdown or matched budgets, and without clear parity in interaction counts, selection rules, or attacker model capacity. Given the evaluation inflation issue above (top-100-on-\(T_{tst}\)), the baseline set is not strong enough to substantiate the magnitude of the claimed improvements.

---

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction
- The paper repeatedly claims it targets “high exploitability and high severity,” but *severity is not measured* beyond a binary harmfulness classifier (Llama-Guard2) (Sec. 3.1). There is no severity gradation, nor evidence that discovered strategies are “more exploitable” in the sense defined (ease for “normal prompt” users) rather than just being more successful under their AMr instantiation. This is a mismatch between the framing and what is evaluated.
- The “exploitability” concept is operationalized as “strategy generalizes across intents,” but that is not the same as “ease to trigger by ordinary users” (Intro’s definition).

### Section 2 (Method)
- Sec. 2.2 introduces a hierarchical decomposition (AMg produces “strategy,” AMr rephrases per intent). This is plausible, but the paper does not clearly specify what constitutes the *action space* of AMg in RL terms (token-level? whole-strategy generation per episode?), nor how AMr is controlled/frozen, which matters for credit assignment and for interpreting “strategy-level exploration.”
- Sec. 2.3.2 (DSP): Equation (3) effectively changes the objective by gating safety reward behind constraint satisfaction and adding penalties otherwise. The claim that “optimal policy … coincides” if penalty is “sufficiently small” is counterintuitive: in early-termination CMDP reductions, *penalty magnitude and termination design* are crucial; “small” penalties can make constraint violations cheap, changing the optimum. This needs either a correct statement of the condition or an argument tailored to their gating design (currently it reads like a misapplication of a theorem).
- Sec. 2.3.3 (PRT): The construction of multiple downgraded models “by ether tuning or in-context learning” is underspecified. If some downgrade models are produced by fine-tuning on toxic data, that is a very strong assumption for “black-box” applicability; for the black-box case they switch to ICL, but then TM′ is not a model, it’s a prompting scheme—this affects FIR definition and comparability.

### Section 3 (Experiments)
- **Most serious**: Eq. (6) and the “top-100 strategies with the highest ASR on \(T_{tst}\)” selection breaks the train/test separation (see Critical Issue 1).
- Dataset split: intents are split into halves (Sec. 3.1), but no evidence is shown that the split is stratified by category or difficulty; given HarmBench’s heterogeneity, results could be split-sensitive. No variance across multiple splits/seeds is reported.
- Safety evaluation: relying on Llama-Guard2-8B as the sole judge is standard, but the paper does not report calibration/false positive/false negative checks on the specific models attacked. This matters because the headline improvements are often single-digit ASR for stronger targets (e.g., Llama2-chat); small judge shifts can change conclusions.
- Table 1 is difficult to interpret due to parsing, but there appear to be inconsistent rows (e.g., Gemma 2 9B shows a very large AUTO-RT ASR relative to others; Mistral row seems garbled). Assuming the original is clean, the review concern is: the paper should report *confidence intervals* or at least std over multiple runs, especially because RL is high-variance and they also claim AUTO-RT has higher within-stage variance (Sec. 3.2).
- Diversity metric SeD: “pairwise semantic similarity” (Sec. 3.1) depends heavily on the embedding model and normalization; not specified here. Also, “lower is better” is fine, but no link is made between SeD and actual functional diversity (i.e., different bypass mechanisms), so conclusions should be cautious.
- Black-box setting (Sec. 3.3.4, Table 4): It reports improvements, but again depends on the same selection/evaluation issue; also unclear whether query budgets are identical across methods and whether defenses/guardrails (rate limits, refusal policies) affect interaction counts.

### Section 4 (Related Work)
- Reasonable categorization, but the paper’s own baselines do not reflect the breadth described here; it reads as if positioned against a larger set than is actually tested in the main controlled experiments.

### Conclusions / Ethics / Reproducibility
- Ethics statement is minimal: given this is a jailbreak strategy search method, a credible release plan (what will be open-sourced, what safeguards, how to prevent misuse) materially affects acceptance in many venues; at least a threat model and mitigation plan should be explicit.

---

## Strengths

- The hierarchical “strategy then rephrase per intent” formulation (Sec. 2.2) is a sensible way to separate reusable jailbreak structure from intent content, and it could be a real contribution if evaluated with a correct protocol.
- Addressing sparse reward in jailbreak discovery via intermediate signals is a meaningful direction; FIR is an interesting attempt to formalize “pick a not-too-weak proxy.”
- The paper evaluates across many target models (claimed 16 white-box + 2 black-box), which—if paired with a sound evaluation protocol—would be a strong empirical base.

---

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Fix the selection protocol**: select strategies using only \(T_{trn}\) (or a dedicated validation intent set), then evaluate once on \(T_{tst}\). Report both “best-of-K” under a fixed sampling budget and “expected” performance (e.g., average over sampled strategies) to avoid selection bias.  
2. **Seed/split robustness**: repeat across multiple random intent splits and PPO seeds; report mean±std/CI for ASR/SeD/DeD.  
3. **Judge robustness**: validate results with at least one additional safety judge and/or a small human audit on a stratified sample to ensure ASR changes aren’t judge artifacts.

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Does PRT preserve the TM objective?** Quantify \(P(R_{TM}=1\mid R_{TM’}=0)\) and \(P(R_{TM}=1\mid R_{TM’}=1)\) across downgrade levels; show cases where TM′ misleads and how FIR avoids them.  
2. **Exploitability vs severity**: if the claim is about both, introduce severity tiers (HarmBench categories, harm magnitude labels, or graded judge) and show AUTO-RT changes the *distribution* of harms, not just binary ASR.

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. Provide qualitative clusters of discovered strategies (e.g., roleplay, translation, obfuscation, tool-use, indirect instruction) and show novelty relative to baselines; otherwise “strategy exploration” is hard to assess.  
2. For DSP, show pruning statistics: what fraction of branches are cut by which constraint, and how that correlates with final success.

### Obvious Next Steps
1. A clean ablation separating “hierarchical strategy modeling” from “DSP/PRT”: i.e., compare query-level RL vs strategy-level RL under identical budgets to substantiate contribution (3) (“strategy-level exploration is essential”).

---

## Overall Assessment

The paper’s core empirical claims do not currently hold because the primary effectiveness metric performs strategy selection on the test set (Sec. 3.1, Eq. 6), which can substantially inflate reported ASR and can differentially advantage methods like AUTO-RT that generate many diverse candidates. Additionally, DeD is not sufficiently specified to support the diversity/coverage conclusions, and the non-potential reward shaping via a downgraded model is justified heuristically without strong evidence that it optimizes the desired target-model jailbreak objective. These are not minor clarifications; they undermine the central “significantly improves success rates / expands coverage” conclusion as presently demonstrated. Based on the current submission, I would not recommend acceptance.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Demonstrates consistent, cross-family gains in attack success over multiple automated baselines**: In the main white-box evaluation (Table 1), AUTO-RT achieves the highest ASR\_tst for most of the 16 target models (e.g., Llama 3 8B Instruct: 14.55 vs RL 7.18; Yi 9B: 39.75 vs RL 34.23; Qwen1.5 7B: 32.60 vs RL 18.82), supporting the claim that strategy-level RL exploration improves jailbreak discovery beyond fixed sampling/IL/RL baselines.
2. **Provides direct evidence that the two proposed techniques materially drive the improvements (not just “RL works”)**: The ablation (Table 2) shows both Dynamic Strategy Pruning (+DSP) and Progressive Reward Tracking (+PRT) improve ASR\_tst over RL on the same models, and their combination (AUTO-RT) yields the largest gains (e.g., Vicuna-7B ASR\_tst: RL 31.95 → +DSP 36.54 → +PRT 40.50 → AUTO-RT 56.40), substantiating that the paper’s specific exploration/reward-shaping mechanisms are doing work.
3. **Shows improved “continuous discovery” via second-round robustness-to-defense metric**: The Defense Generalization Diversity (DeD) column in Table 1 and Table 2 indicates AUTO-RT’s strategies remain more effective after defenses are constructed from first-round successes (e.g., Vicuna-13B DeD: AUTO-RT 56.33 vs RL 21.03; R2D2 DeD: AUTO-RT 41.78 vs RL 4.33), aligning with the paper’s claim of broader vulnerability coverage and sustained exploration vs prior automated methods that overfit narrow templates.
4. **Introduces and empirically validates FIR as a concrete selection rule for reward-shaping downgrade models**: Section 2.3.3 defines FIR and Figure 4 reports that choosing “the last model before a sharp rise in FIR” yields the best attack ASR, providing actionable evidence that their non-potential-based reward shaping (Eq. 4–5) can be made reliable via the proposed metric rather than arbitrary downgrade-model choice.
5. **Establishes that the approach extends beyond white-box by adapting downgrade construction to black-box constraints**: In the black-box setup (Table 4), AUTO-RT with an in-context-learning-built downgrade model substantially improves ASR\_tst over FS/IL/RL for both targets (Llama3-70B: 14.88 vs RL 4.99; Qwen2.5-72B: 14.47 vs RL 4.53), evidencing the claim that AUTO-RT can operate when fine-tuning the target is infeasible.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Clear formalization of “strategic red-teaming” as a hierarchical optimization problem that explicitly targets exploitability**: The decomposition into strategy generation AM\_g and strategy rephrasing AM\_r (Section 2.2) with the rewritten objective (Eq. 2) is a concrete modeling step that differs from prior template-limited systems the paper cites (e.g., AutoDAN/Rainbow/PAIR) and sets up an exploration space where transferable strategies can be learned and evaluated across intents.
2. **Efficiency claim is backed by training-dynamics evidence rather than only final ASR**: The paper evaluates ASR\_tst over training stages and reports AUTO-RT outperforming RL at each stage with higher within-stage variance suggesting broader exploration (Figure 3), which is the kind of evidence needed to support “accelerates discovery” beyond endpoint comparisons.
3. **Competes favorably with strong human-template baselines on sustained performance, not just first-round ASR**: Table 3 shows AUTO-RT achieving the highest DeD (38.19) compared to AutoDAN (17.88), Human Template (13.15), and Past-Tense (7.27), supporting the paper’s claim of “near-human-level sustained attack capabilities” specifically in a setting meant to penalize brittle, template-like strategies.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Pragmatic, implementable design choices for constraints and evaluation**: Section 3.1 specifies concrete constraint mechanisms (CRT-style diversity penalty; LLM-based consistency judge) and a standard safety evaluator (Llama-Guard2-8B), making the framework closer to an end-to-end red-teaming recipe than many prior works that leave key components underspecified.

## Summary

The paper proposes AUTO-RT, an RL framework for strategy-level jailbreak exploration that improves automated red-teaming beyond fixed strategy/template methods via Dynamic Strategy Pruning and Progressive Reward Tracking (with FIR-guided downgrade-model selection). The most important evidence that it delivers is the large, consistent improvement in white-box results across many model families in Table 1—paired with the ablation in Table 2 showing DSP and PRT each contribute and jointly produce the best ASR\_tst/DeD—demonstrating both effectiveness and sustained vulnerability discovery after defenses are applied.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper proposes **AUTO-RT**, an RL framework for *strategy-level* jailbreak discovery via a hierarchical attacker (strategy generator + intent rephraser), with two additions: **Dynamic Strategy Pruning (DSP)** for early termination under constraints and **Progressive Reward Tracking (PRT)** that shapes rewards using a “downgraded” target model selected by the proposed **First Inverse Rate (FIR)**. Experiments report higher attack success and improved second-round performance after defenses across many target LLMs.

## Strengths
- **Clear hierarchical “strategy → rephrase per intent” formulation with an explicit objective** (Sec. 2.2, Eq. (2)), which is a sensible way to separate reusable jailbreak structure from intent instantiation.
- **Large empirical sweep with ablations showing DSP/PRT each matter**: Table 2 reports consistent ASR\_tst gains from adding +DSP and/or +PRT over RL, with the combined method highest on the shown targets (Sec. 3.3.1, Table 2).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
- **Test-set selection leakage in the headline metric (inflates reported ASR\_tst gains).** The paper defines effectiveness as “**average ASR of the top 100 strategies with the highest ASR on T\_tst**” (Sec. 3.1, Eq. (6): “top 100 strategies with the highest ASR on Ttst”). This is selecting strategies *using the test set and evaluating on that same test set*, which systematically overestimates performance and can disproportionately benefit methods that generate more/high-variance candidates. This directly undermines claims like “improves success rates (by up to 16.63%)” because the reported numbers are not a clean generalization estimate.

### Major
- **DeD (“Defense Generalization Diversity”) is too underspecified to support “diversity/coverage/continuous discovery” conclusions.** DeD is defined only as “constructing defenses based on the successful attacks, and evaluating the ASR\_tst of second-round attacks on the defended model” (Sec. 3.1). The paper excerpt does not specify what the defense procedure is, whether the defense budget is fixed, how many successful attacks per method are used to build the defense, nor whether hyperparameters/base model are held constant. Without an equal-budget, precisely-defined defender, DeD cannot be reliably interpreted as a diversity/coverage metric, and related claims in Sec. 3.2/3.3 are not well-supported.
- **DSP theoretical claim appears incorrect/mis-stated.** In Sec. 2.3.2, after introducing the gated/penalized objective (Eq. (3)), the paper claims: “**When the penalty C(fi,ci) is sufficiently small … the optimal policy … coincides with that of the original CMDP**” (lines 66–68). As written, “sufficiently small” is counterintuitive for guaranteeing equivalence; this needs correction/clarification (conditions, sign conventions, or a tailored argument for their specific gating/termination scheme).
- **PRT/FIR reward shaping remains heuristic with limited evidence that it preserves the target-model objective.** The paper explicitly notes the shaping is not potential-based (Sec. 2.3.3), so policy/strategy rankings can change. The key empirical justification (“most cases with R\_{TM’}=0 also yield R\_{TM}=0”) is asserted but not quantified in the excerpt (lines 78–84), and FIR selection is based on a “sharp increase” heuristic (lines 111–113, Sec. 3.3.2). This leaves a real risk that optimization targets the proxy (TM′) in ways that do not preserve best jailbreak strategies on TM.

### Minor
- **Exploitability/severity framing is not matched by the evaluation as described.** The introduction emphasizes *exploitability* and *severity* (Sec. 1), but the experimental metrics in Sec. 3.1 center on ASR judged by Llama-Guard2-8B and “exploitability” is effectively operationalized as strategy generalization across intents; severity is not clearly measured on a graded scale in the provided text.
- **Intent split robustness is not established.** The dataset is split into two halves (T\_trn/T\_tst) (Sec. 3.1), but the excerpt does not report multiple random splits/seeds or variance estimates; given RL variance and heterogeneous intents, split sensitivity could matter.

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves
- Report an additional judge or small human audit to sanity-check that small ASR differences are not judge artifacts (given single-judge evaluation in Sec. 3.1).

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“Table 1 seems garbled/inconsistent rows”**: the user notes formatting artifacts from PDF extraction; this is not a reliable basis for criticism.
- **“Need confidence intervals / multiple seeds” as a requirement**: useful, but I kept only the split/variance concern as *minor*; demanding CIs is not necessarily standard for every large benchmark setting.

## Novel Insights
A key mismatch is that the paper’s *core* quantitative claim (“up to 16.63%”) rests on an evaluation definition (Eq. (6)) that is effectively “best-of-K on the test set,” which is not merely a statistical nit but a protocol error that also entangles with the paper’s narrative about “exploitability” (general strategies) and “coverage” (DeD). Fixing the protocol (strategy selection on train/val only, then one-shot test evaluation) would likely change not just absolute ASR, but also the relative benefit attributed to exploration mechanisms (DSP/PRT), since those mechanisms can increase candidate variance and thus profit more from test-set selection.

## Suggestions
- Redefine effectiveness to avoid test leakage: select strategies using **T\_trn** (or a dedicated validation set), then evaluate **once** on **T\_tst**; additionally report “expected ASR” under a fixed sampling budget alongside any best-of-K metric.
- Fully specify the DeD defense pipeline (algorithm, budget, number of training examples per method, hyperparameters, and whether the defense uses identical resources across methods); consider reporting DeD under multiple fixed defense budgets.
- Correct/clarify the DSP equivalence statement around Eq. (3) (what conditions on penalties/termination ensure equivalence, if any).
- Quantify proxy-to-target agreement for PRT (e.g., empirical conditional rates linking TM′ outcomes to TM outcomes across downgrade levels) and show that FIR choice preserves ranking of strategies on TM.

## Score and Decision
**Calibration note:** I was unable to retrieve calibration anchors because `calibration_search` repeatedly failed with an internal “invalid model ID” error, so I cannot provide the required high/medium/low human-score comparisons.

Given the **fatal evaluation protocol leakage in Eq. (6)**, the main empirical claim is not currently supported in a way that meets ICLR standards, despite a promising problem and reasonable-looking ablations.

