### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **Ill-posed / non-identifiable inverse “recovery” is treated as ground-truth inference (Structural).**  
   - **Where:** Abstract (“accurate recovery of latent coefficients”), Sec. 1 (inverse problems without paired data), Sec. 3.2 (training \(\varphi(x_1)=\alpha_1\) by minimizing PDE residual), Sec. 4 (reports MMD\(_\alpha\) to a synthetic reference).  
   - **Why it breaks central claims:** Training \(\varphi\) *only* by minimizing \(\mathcal R_{\text{weak}}(x,\alpha)\) does not identify the true latent parameter in general: many PDE families admit non-unique \(\alpha\) given \(x\) (or near-non-uniqueness under noise/model mismatch), and weak residual minimization will select an arbitrary member of an equivalence class (often biased toward “easy” parameters under the chosen parameterization/regularization). Yet the paper repeatedly frames this as “recovery” of hidden physical inputs and evaluates \(\alpha\) against a *synthetic* reference distribution \(D_{\text{ref}}\) (Sec. 4), which silently reintroduces the supervised setting the method claims to avoid. Unless the authors formalize identifiability assumptions (and verify them per PDE) or explicitly reposition the contribution as “finds *some* parameter consistent with PDE + generator,” the inverse-problem headline claim is not supported.

2) **Evaluation protocol conflates “physics enforcement” with “moving toward a different dataset” (Evidential).**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 4 (definition of \(D_{\text{ref}}\); residuals scaled by mean residual of \(D_{\text{ref}}\); MMD computed against \(D_{\text{ref}}\)); Helmholtz/Stokes/Elasticity are explicitly *misspecified* at fine-tuning time (Secs. 4.3–4.5).  
   - **Why it undermines conclusions:** The paper fine-tunes toward a *new* PDE specification (changed BC amplitude; damped→lossless; forced→unforced) and then evaluates distributional similarity and parameter MMD **to a synthetic dataset generated under that new specification**. This makes improvements ambiguous: the method may simply be doing dataset translation from the training distribution (observational/noisy/misspecified) toward \(D_{\text{ref}}\), and MMD-to-\(D_{\text{ref}}\) is then tautologically aligned with the optimization target. This does not establish the claimed property “without distorting the underlying learned distribution” (Abstract; Sec. 1; Sec. 5), because “underlying” is unclear: the original observational distribution or the target PDE-consistent distribution? As written, the evaluation does not disentangle (i) reduced residual, (ii) loss of diversity / mode dropping, and (iii) distribution shift relative to the *original* data distribution.

3) **The “joint evolution” over \(\alpha_t\) is a heuristic surrogate without demonstrated correctness or necessity (Evidential → close to Structural for the inverse claim).**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 3.2 (“surrogate base flow” for \(\alpha\) built from \(\hat \alpha_1=\varphi(\hat x_1)\); \(v^{\text{base}}_{t,\alpha}=(\hat\alpha_1-\alpha_t)/(1-t)\)); Sec. 4 comparisons (“Base AM”, “Base AM+\(\varphi\)”, “AM joint”).  
   - **Why it matters:** The base model has no learned \(\alpha\)-process; the paper manufactures one by (a) taking a one-step Euler proxy \(\hat x_1=x_t+(1-t) v^{\text{base}}_t(x_t)\) and (b) mapping it through \(\varphi\). There is no argument that this produces a meaningful conditional path distribution over \(\alpha_t\), nor that the resulting control problem corresponds to tilting a well-defined joint density \(p(x,\alpha)\). Consequently, claims that the method “generates latent parameters alongside states” in a principled stochastic-control sense (Sec. 1 contributions; Sec. 3.2–3.3) are not established; it could simply be an engineered regularizer that happens to help some metrics. The experiments suggest gains in MMD\(_\alpha\) (Sec. 4.5), but do not validate that \(\alpha\)-samples represent a posterior, are identifiable, or are robust to alternative parameterizations of \(\alpha\).

4) **Key theoretical conditions for adjoint-matching consistency are violated/unclear in the actual objective used (Methodological gap, but impacts how much one should trust the “theoretical grounding” contribution).**  
   - **Where:** Sec. 3.3 (states consistency “with \(f=0\)” and memoryless schedule; but later introduces a nonzero running cost \(f(\alpha)=\lambda_f\|v^{ft}_{t,\alpha}-v^{reg}_{t,\alpha}\|^2\)); also reports samples generated with \(\sigma(t)=0\) although training uses memoryless \(\sigma(t)\) (Sec. 4).  
   - **Why it matters:** The paper advertises theoretical grounding via tilted distributions, but then (i) adds an extra running cost that is not part of the original guarantee, and (ii) evaluates by *deterministic* sampling (no noise) even though the memoryless property/mixing is central to the derivation. This doesn’t necessarily make the method invalid, but it weakens the “principled” claim: the actual algorithm is a hybrid of adjoint matching + bespoke regularization + deterministic inference, and the paper does not analyze what distribution it targets in that setting.

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Introduction
- The abstract’s phrasing “accurate recovery of latent coefficients” and “without distorting the underlying learned distribution” is stronger than what the method/evaluation actually justifies, given the identifiability and evaluation issues above.
- The motivation “no paired parameter–solution data” conflicts with later reliance on synthetic \(D_{\text{ref}}\) for parameter-distribution evaluation (Sec. 4).

### Related Work
- Generally solid positioning versus physics-informed diffusion, projection methods, and PBFM.  
- The discussion suggests the paper’s main differentiator is *post-training* enforcement + *latent parameter inference without pairs*; that second part is where the current evidence is weakest.

### Method (Sec. 3)
- The weak-form residual construction is plausible and likely a practical contribution (Sec. 3.1), but the paper does not specify how sensitive results are to the stochastic test-function design (choice of basis, support radii distribution, \(N_{\text{test}}\)), which could materially affect optimization and comparisons.
- Sec. 3.2’s surrogate \(\alpha\)-flow is under-justified: it mixes a crude one-step terminal estimator \(\hat x_1\) with a learned inverse \(\varphi\) that itself is trained only by residual minimization. This composes two approximations and then treats the result as a “base trajectory” for \(\alpha\).
- Sec. 3.3: Eq. (3) as presented appears to use repeated \(J_{xx}\) blocks (likely a parsing artifact), but the bigger issue is conceptual: the “theoretical” link to reward-tilting is stated for \(f=0\) and a specific stochastic setting, whereas the practical algorithm uses extra costs and then reports deterministic sampling.

### Experiments (Sec. 4)
- The paper often evaluates “distributional fidelity” via MMD to \(D_{\text{ref}}\), not to the *training* distribution. This makes it hard to substantiate “preserving diversity” in the sense a generative-model audience expects.
- Helmholtz table: selecting “representative configurations” as the ones minimizing either residual or MMD (Table 2) makes the comparison feel tuned per method; without a fixed tuning protocol/budget across methods, it is hard to interpret.
- Stokes: stating “PBFM fails to converge” without showing a fair tuning effort/budget (beyond an appendix pointer) risks an unfair baseline impression; because this is used to strengthen the narrative, it needs especially careful standardization.
- Natural images: the “cross-domain utility” demo is interesting but not a strong validation of the physics-specific claims; it mainly shows the method can optimize a reward with an extra parametric transformation. Also, it’s unclear what the “parameter inference” means here (since \(\alpha\) is an imposed transform, not a hidden physical variable).

## Strengths

- Clear attempt to unify post-training constraint enforcement for FM models with adjoint-matching control, and to use weak-form residuals that are often more stable than strong-form PDE penalties (Sec. 3.1).
- The empirical knob \((\lambda_x,\lambda_\alpha,\lambda_f,\kappa)\) for trading residual reduction vs. distribution shift is practically useful, and Darcy ablations (Sec. 4.1, Fig. 3) illustrate the behavior.
- Addressing misspecification scenarios (changed BCs; damped→lossless; forced→unforced) is a relevant and harder regime than “train and test on same PDE.”

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Identifiability stress tests for \(\alpha\):** construct cases where multiple \(\alpha\) yield similar residual for a given \(x\) (or near-symmetries), and show whether inferred \(\alpha\) is stable or arbitrary; without this, “latent coefficient recovery” is not credible (Secs. 3.2, 4).  
2. **Evaluate distribution preservation against the *original* data distribution:** report MMD (or alternative) to the base training data in addition to \(D_{\text{ref}}\); otherwise “preserve diversity / avoid distortion” is unsubstantiated (Sec. 4).  
3. **A fair, fixed-budget hyperparameter protocol across baselines:** especially for Table 2’s “representative configs” and claims like “PBFM fails to converge” (Secs. 4.4–4.5).

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **What joint distribution is being learned over \((x,\alpha)\)?** clarify whether the target is a posterior, a joint energy model \(p(x,\alpha)\propto p_{\text{base}}(x)\exp(-\lambda \mathcal R(x,\alpha))\), or something else—because the surrogate \(\alpha\)-flow makes this non-obvious (Sec. 3.2–3.3).  
2. **Sensitivity of weak residual to test-function sampling:** demonstrate robustness to \(\Psi\), \(N_{\text{test}}\), kernel supports; otherwise residual reductions may be partly an artifact of the probe distribution (Sec. 3.1).

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Parameter ambiguity examples:** show multiple distinct \(\alpha\) fields that achieve similar residual for the same generated \(x\), and where \(\varphi\) lands among them (Secs. 3.2, 4.1).

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Reframe inverse claim as “consistent parameterization” unless identifiability is proven/validated:** the current framing oversells what residual-only training can guarantee.

## Overall Assessment

The paper has a promising practical recipe (weak-form residuals + adjoint-matching-style post-training) and some convincing residual reductions under misspecification, but its central inverse-problem claim—recovering latent PDE parameters without paired data—is not currently supported: the proposed \(\varphi\) training is fundamentally non-identifying in general, and the evaluation leans on a synthetic reference distribution that blurs the line between unsupervised inverse inference and supervised target matching. Combined with a gap between the stated theoretical grounding and the actual objective/sampling used, these issues are serious enough that the submission, in its current form, does not establish its headline contributions.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Physics enforcement is done post-training and demonstrably improves physical validity while controlling distribution shift**: In Darcy (Sec. 4.1, Fig. 2–3), fine-tuning reduces visible noise and improves coherence, and the ablations in Fig. 3 explicitly show how increasing \(\lambda_x=\lambda_\alpha\) lowers PDE residuals while affecting diversity, and how \(\lambda_f\) trades off residual reduction vs MMD-to-base (distributional fidelity).
2. **Joint state–parameter generation materially outperforms “use-\(\varphi\)-only” ablations on inverse problems under misspecification**: In Stokes (Sec. 4.5, Fig. 5), the key claimed benefit of the *joint flow over \((x,\alpha)\)* is supported by substantially better parameter-distribution alignment (reported \( \mathrm{MMD}_\alpha \approx 0.07\!-\!0.13\) for the joint model vs \(\approx 0.22\!-\!0.28\) for Base AM variants) even when residual levels are similar—evidence that modeling \(\alpha_t\) dynamically adds capability beyond simply predicting \(\alpha\) at the end.
3. **Weak-form residual design is evidenced as practically stabilizing across multiple PDE families (including BCs) rather than being a purely conceptual choice**: The method’s reward uses weak residuals with random local test functions (Sec. 3.1), and the experiments evaluate both weak and *strong* residuals; e.g., in Linear Elasticity (Sec. 4.3, Table 1), the approach simultaneously achieves very low BC error (1.71e−6) and low strong residual (3.79×10\(^0\)) with low MMD\(_x\) (0.15), indicating the weak-form training signal transfers to strong-form physical correctness and boundary adherence better than the compared alternatives.

### Supporting strengths
1. **The scaled “memoryless” noise schedule is a concrete, paper-specific extension with both theoretical and empirical role**: Sec. 3.3 introduces \(\sigma^2(t)=(1-\kappa)2\eta_t\) and claims (with Lemma 1 in App. D.4) this preserves the memoryless property while improving numerical stability; the experiments explicitly motivate and use \(\kappa>0\) for PDE pixel-space models due to high-variance sampling noise (Sec. 4), showing the new degree of freedom is used as intended rather than remaining a theoretical aside.
2. **Head-to-head comparisons show a favorable residual–distribution trade-off relative to prior physics-constrained FM training**: In Linear Elasticity (Table 1), compared to PBFM, the method attains comparable residuals (Rweak 6.15 vs 6.32) but dramatically lower distribution shift (MMD\(_x\) 0.15 vs 0.92) and much lower BC error (1.71e−6 vs 2.32e−5), evidencing “constraint satisfaction without distorting the learned distribution” better than that baseline.
3. **Robustness to controlled model mismatch is directly tested and the method remains best-in-metrics**: In Helmholtz (Sec. 4.4, Table 2), with damped training data but lossless fine-tuning target, the joint AM model achieves the lowest weak residual (4.3×10\(^0\)) *and* the lowest MMD\(_x\) (0.06) among all listed methods/configs, supporting the claim that the approach handles misspecification while preserving sample fidelity.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Clear, multi-metric evaluation protocol makes “physics vs diversity” measurable rather than anecdotal**: Sec. 4 defines normalized weak/strong residuals against a clean reference set \(D_{\mathrm{ref}}\) and reports MMD for both states and parameters (MMD\(_x\), MMD\(_\alpha\)), enabling the paper’s central trade-off claims to be checked quantitatively (e.g., Fig. 3; Tables 1–2; Fig. 5).
2. **Practicality is evidenced by lightweight adaptation cost and no inference-time correction**: For noisy Darcy, the paper reports fine-tuning in 20 gradient steps, under 15 minutes on a single NVIDIA L40S, and notes sampling is done at base-model cost with \(\sigma(t)=0\) (Sec. 4.1, Sec. 4), supporting the “post-training” pitch as operationally attractive compared to projection-at-inference schemes.

## Summary

The paper proposes post-training physics-constrained fine-tuning for flow-matching models that (i) enforces PDE constraints via weak-form residual rewards and adjoint matching and (ii) enables inverse problems by evolving latent parameters jointly with the state. The strongest evidence it delivers on this is the inverse-problem misspecification result on Stokes (Sec. 4.5, Fig. 5), where the joint \((x,\alpha)\) flow achieves markedly better parameter-distribution alignment (much lower MMD\(_\alpha\)) than non-joint adjoint-matching ablations at similar residual levels, indicating the method’s core “joint generation + physics constraint” mechanism adds real capability beyond prior post-hoc or non-joint approaches.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper proposes **physics-constrained post-training fine-tuning** for flow-matching generative models using **weak-form PDE residuals** as rewards within an **Adjoint Matching** control formulation, and extends the generator to a **joint evolution over states and latent PDE parameters** to support inverse problems without paired parameter–solution training data. Experiments across Darcy, elasticity, Helmholtz, Stokes (with deliberate misspecification), plus a natural-image demo, report reduced residuals and improved distributional metrics relative to several baselines/ablations.

## Strengths
- **Clear, concrete post-training recipe with measurable trade-offs**: Darcy ablations explicitly show how \((\lambda_x,\lambda_\alpha,\lambda_f)\) move along residual vs. distribution-shift axes (Sec. 4.1, Fig. 3), and the method is positioned as lightweight adaptation (20 steps; Sec. 4.1).
- **Empirical evidence that the joint \((x,\alpha)\) modeling helps beyond end-of-trajectory \(\varphi\)**: In Stokes, residual levels across AM variants are similar, but only the joint model achieves much lower \( \mathrm{MMD}_\alpha\) (Sec. 4.5, Fig. 5 discussion; lines 203–205).
- **Weak-form residual rationale is articulated and used consistently**: The paper motivates weak residuals as more stable than strong-form penalties and defines a stochastic test-function scheme (Sec. 3.1, lines 50–58), then reports both weak/strong residual metrics in PDE experiments (Sec. 4).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **Inverse “recovery” claims are stronger than what the method identifies**: The paper repeatedly frames \(\varphi(x_1)=\alpha_1\) trained *only* by minimizing \(\mathcal R_{\text{weak}}(x,\alpha)\) as “accurate recovery of latent coefficients” (Abstract, line 10; Sec. 3.2, lines 62–64). But the method as written does not state identifiability conditions (e.g., when \(\alpha\mapsto \mathcal{L}_\alpha x\) is injective given the observation model), so “recovery” is not guaranteed—at best it finds parameters that make residuals small under the chosen weak probes and regularizers. This matters because it is part of the headline contribution and affects how to interpret \( \mathrm{MMD}_\alpha\) improvements as “correct inference” vs. “self-consistent parameterization.”
- **Distributional-fidelity evaluation is anchored to a synthetic *target* reference set, not the original data distribution**: The paper defines \(D_{\text{ref}}\) as “a synthetic, clean dataset generated under the target PDE specification assumed during fine-tuning” and computes both residual normalization and MMDs against it (Sec. 4, lines 141–142). This makes “without distorting the underlying learned distribution” (Abstract, line 10; Intro contribution, line 15) ambiguous: the reported MMD\(_x\) primarily measures closeness to the *target* (clean/misspecification-adjusted) distribution rather than preservation of the *original observational/training* distribution. The paper partially addresses this in Darcy by also reporting MMD to the **base dataset** in an ablation panel (Sec. 4.1, line 149), but the core multi-PDE tables/results emphasize \(D_{\text{ref}}\), leaving the “preservation” claim under-supported outside that one setting.
- **The “theoretical grounding” is only partially aligned with the practical objective/sampling used**: Sec. 3.3 explicitly notes that the adjoint-matching consistency claim holds “with \(f=0\)” and memoryless noise (lines 127–128), but the method then adds a nonzero running cost \(f(\alpha)=\lambda_f\|v^{ft}_{t,\alpha}-v^{reg}_{t,\alpha}\|^2\) (lines 134–137) and reports results generated with **deterministic sampling** \(\sigma(t)=0\) (Sec. 4, line 141). These choices may be reasonable heuristics, but they weaken the advertised “principled/consistent tilted-distribution” story as applied to the actual algorithm evaluated.

### Minor
- **Helmholtz “representative configurations” selection muddies comparisons**: Table 2 reports “representative configurations…selected as either the setting with the lowest weak residual or the lowest MMD\(_x\)” (Sec. 4.4, lines 194–195). This can overstate performance if different methods get to choose different hyperparameter points for different metrics; a fixed selection protocol (single budget/criterion) would make the comparison cleaner.
- **Weak-residual test-function sampling is under-specified in the main text and lacks robustness evidence**: Sec. 3.1 defines random local polynomial kernels and references Appendix D.3 for details (lines 54–58), but the main paper does not show sensitivity to \(N_{\text{test}}\), kernel supports, or sampling distributions—parameters that could materially affect optimization and reported residual reductions.

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves
- Add a targeted **identifiability / ambiguity stress test** for \(\alpha\) (e.g., construct cases with multiple \(\alpha\) producing near-equal weak residuals for a fixed \(x\)) and report stability of inferred \(\alpha\) under reparameterizations and noise.
- For each PDE, report an explicit **MMD-to-training-data** (or other distributional distance to the base data) alongside MMD-to-\(D_{\text{ref}}\) to substantiate “preservation” across all settings, not only Darcy.

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“Eq. (3) has repeated \(J_{xx}\) blocks”**: the text itself suggests this is likely a parsing artifact; I did not treat this as a substantive weakness.
- **Any concern about release/availability or unverifiability of cited methods/datasets**: not applicable per instructions.
- **Generic “missing related work” / formatting/typo complaints**: removed by policy.

## Novel Insights
A core tension in the paper is that it simultaneously (i) markets a **tilted-distribution, theoretically grounded control view** and (ii) uses several pragmatic departures (extra running cost on \(\alpha\), deterministic sampling, surrogate \(\alpha\)-base flow via \(\hat x_1\) and \(\varphi\)). Tightening the paper’s framing—by clearly separating what is theoretically justified vs. what is heuristic but empirically effective—would likely improve credibility more than adding yet another PDE benchmark.

## Suggestions
- Rephrase claims from “accurate recovery” to **“PDE-consistent parameter estimation”** unless you (a) state identifiability assumptions per PDE family and (b) empirically validate them.
- Standardize evaluation to include **two distributional references** everywhere: distance to the **original/base data distribution** (to support “preservation”) and distance to **\(D_{\text{ref}}\)** (to support “target physics”).
- In Helmholtz/Stokes, report results under a **single fixed hyperparameter selection rule** (e.g., selected on a held-out set by one scalar objective) rather than “representative configs.”

## Score and Decision
**Calibration:** Unable to run `calibration_search` due to a tool backend/model-ID error (“not a valid model ID”), so I cannot provide the required high/medium/low human-score anchors. Interpreting the paper on its own merits: it has a solid and useful post-training physics-enforcement mechanism with credible empirical improvements, but it **overclaims** on inverse “recovery” and its distribution-preservation story is not consistently supported by the chosen reference-based evaluation. These are substantial but plausibly fixable with reframing + additional analyses/metrics; still, as written, the headline claims are not fully backed.

