### Harsh Critic
## Critical Issues

1) **Structural — The paper’s headline “blueprint” contribution is not actually instantiated as a general, actionable methodology.**  
   The abstract/introduction claim a “blueprint for performing more meticulous and rigorous science,” but the body largely delivers a single adversarial re-review of one target paper plus a small set of familiar maxims (control tuning, correct p-values, transparency). There is no formalized protocol (e.g., decision procedure, checklists with stopping conditions, preregistration template, reporting standard, or audited pipeline) that would let others reproduce the *blueprint* beyond “do what we did here.” This breaks the central framing as more than a commentary/case-study. (Abstract; Sec. 1; Sec. 6 “General Lessons”; the “novel methodology” claim around “Best-of-N” in Sec. 3 is not elevated into a general standard with guarantees/limits.)

2) **Evidential — Several strongest allegations rely on under-documented provenance (author communications, “Telegram link,” “publicly confirmed,” “ongoing work”) in ways that are pivotal to the conclusions but not verifiable from the paper’s presented evidence.**  
   Claims like omitted baseline “publicly confirmed with the authors” (Sec. 2.1), selective reporting supported by a “Telegram link” (Sec. 4.3), and adoption claims being “retracted” and reviewers relying on them (Sec. 5) are central to the narrative of invalidation and selective reporting, yet the submission (as provided) does not present these artifacts in a citable, archived, and auditable way inside the paper itself (e.g., hashes/URLs to immutable snapshots, screenshots in appendix, exact queries, timestamps, replication scripts). If these materials are only off-paper and mutable, the evidential basis is weaker than the rhetoric suggests. This does not mean the allegations are false; it means the submission, as a scientific document, does not itself carry the burden of proof for its most serious claims.

3) **Methodological gap — The “Best-of-N controls hyperparameter volume” analysis is directionally sensible but not yet justified as a *fairness principle* rather than a new evaluation objective that can change the question.**  
   In Sec. 3, the core move is: equalize the number of hyperparameter configurations sampled and compare maxima (“best-of”). That is appropriate if the deployment story is “I can afford to tune N configs per method.” But the original claim under dispute could also be interpreted as “min-p yields better outputs *for typical/default settings*” or “has a better Pareto frontier over a continuous control,” in which case “max over N random subsamples” is not the right estimand. The paper doesn’t disambiguate the target estimand (default performance vs tuned performance vs envelope quality-diversity frontier) and then align the metric accordingly; instead it treats “control hyperparameter volume” as obviously the correct fairness criterion. Without that alignment, this part supports a narrower claim: “min-p is not better under equal tuning budget on GSM8K-CoT across the tested grids,” not the broader invalidation tone. (Sec. 3.1–3.2; Figs. 4–5 discussion.)

## Section-by-Section Notes

### Abstract / Sec. 1 Introduction
- The abstract promises a “blueprint” and a “novel methodology” for fair comparison under tuning. What actually appears is (i) a case study, and (ii) one tuning-budget sensitivity analysis (Best-of-N). The gap between framing and delivered generality is substantial.  
- The paper’s tone repeatedly moves from “insufficient evidence” to “invalidated” and “selective reporting” across multiple evidence streams. Given the seriousness, the document needs especially tight internal evidentiary standards (archived artifacts, reproducible scripts, clear attribution of what is in the original paper vs learned via author communication). Right now, some of the most damning points are asserted rather than documented inside the paper.

### Sec. 2 Re-Analyzing Min-p’s Human Evaluations
- **2.1 omitted baseline**: If accurate, this is important; but the submission should precisely specify *where* the omitted data live, how they were linked to the reported study, and how exclusion affects each reported analysis (not just “changes conclusions”). As written, it’s rhetorically strong but procedurally under-specified.
- **2.2 stats**: The critique about pooling across conditions vs per-condition tests is plausible, and the multiple-comparisons point is legitimate. However, the paper adopts one-sided paired t-tests without discussing whether the score distributions meet assumptions, whether the pairing is per-participant across samplers (likely) and whether missingness/attention-check filtering could break pairing. Those details matter to the correctness of the re-test. (Table 1; discussion around IUT.)
- The “Intersection-Union Test” argument is only appropriate if the original paper’s claim is literally “better in all 12 settings” as a statistical claim; many ML papers use “consistently” colloquially. You can still criticize the overclaim, but the IUT framing risks straw-manning unless you quote the exact language precisely and interpret it conservatively. (Sec. 2.2.)
- **2.3 qualitative annotation**: Manual annotation of free-text preferences is fragile; the paper should report inter-annotator agreement and coding rules (even briefly) because this is used to contradict the original qualitative summary. Without that, it’s easy for the re-annotation to be accused of subjective recoding. (Fig. 2 reference.)
- **2.4 new human eval**: This section reads like a post-hoc patchwork by the original authors; your point is that even the new study doesn’t show superiority. But you also allege a numeric misreport (7.80 vs 5.80). That is a serious accusation and needs airtight presentation: show the exact computation, the relevant rows from the released data, and how the mean is formed. As written, it’s a single-sentence claim.

### Sec. 3 Extending Min-p’s NLP Benchmark Evaluations
- The sweep is substantial and the core point—performance advantages can vanish when tuning budgets are equalized—is important.  
- But the analysis focuses only on GSM8K-CoT, while the original paper also claimed results on GPQA and presumably other settings. You acknowledge compute limits, but then the conclusion is stated broadly (“min-p’s claimed superiority vanishes…”). As evidence, this supports *partial* undermining, not comprehensive invalidation across “benchmarks” plural unless the paper clearly bounds the claim. (Sec. 3 opening vs later conclusions.)
- The construction “N = 1 to 100” when each sampler only has 6 non-temperature hyperparameters (plus 31 temperatures) is confusing in the extracted text: it sounds like you are sampling from a pool of configurations (temperature × p/k × seeds), but you describe it as “subsample N hyperparameters.” This needs precise definition of what counts as a “configuration” and what pool size is being sampled from per sampler, otherwise the fairness claim is hard to interpret. (Sec. 3 methodology bullets + analysis description.)

### Sec. 4 Investigating Min-p’s LLM-as-a-Judge Evaluations
- The critique that indirect comparisons against a fixed baseline can be invalid under non-transitive preferences is potentially strong. But you need to show the actual comparison graph used and why it blocks inference for the specific questions the original paper answered. As written, it gestures at the issue without demonstrating the inferential failure formally or empirically (e.g., exhibiting cycles / showing that direct min-p vs top-p comparisons change conclusions). (Sec. 4.1; Xu et al. 2025 cite.)
- The claim that min-p got ~2× more tuning and that CIs overlap is plausible, but again depends on careful, reproducible counting: what is “tuning” in AlpacaEval runs (temperatures? p values? prompts? seeds?), and how are CIs computed for win-rates (paired? bootstrap over prompts?). The paper asserts, but doesn’t fully specify. (Sec. 4.2; Fig. 6.)
- **4.3 selective reporting** is a major integrity allegation. It cannot rest on “a Telegram link” as described; the paper should embed the evidence (archived link, screenshot, or mirrored artifact) and specify exactly what table entry corresponds to which run IDs. Without that, this is not publishable as a scientific claim at the level of accusation made.

### Sec. 5 Community adoption
- The argument about stars arithmetic is not a valid refutation by itself: “leading LM repos sum to 453k stars” does not logically upper-bound stars across 54k repos. The *real* refutation is the false-positive “min-p” search and subsequent author retraction. The section should focus on replicating the exact query methodology and showing measured false-positive rate, rather than using an unrelated comparator (top LM repos). (Sec. 5.)
- The statement that reviewers/AC relied on the adoption numbers is interesting meta-science, but it is ancillary unless you can document it precisely and ethically (quotations are okay if public; otherwise it risks veering into editorializing).

### Sec. 6 Discussion and Limitations
- The limitation (“new evidence might lead to different conclusions”) somewhat conflicts with the paper’s repeated “invalidates” language. Either the submission is a definitive refutation within a clearly bounded scope, or it is a cautionary re-analysis. The rhetoric should match the epistemic stance.

## Strengths

- The paper targets a real, high-impact failure mode in empirical ML: unequal tuning budgets and selective reporting can easily create illusory gains.  
- The human-eval statistical critique (pooling across conditions; multiple comparisons; showing per-setting tests) is substantively useful and, if fully documented, would be a strong pedagogical example. (Sec. 2.2; Table 1.)
- The large-scale GSM8K sweep is a serious investment and, conceptually, the tuning-budget sensitivity curve is a helpful diagnostic to discourage cherry-picking. (Sec. 3; Figs. 4–5.)

## Missing Parts and Places to Improve

### Missing Experiments
1. **Direct LLM-judge pairwise comparisons (min-p vs top-p, min-p vs basic) under the same judge/prompt set** because the current critique hinges on non-transitivity and indirect design; a direct test would make the argument decisive rather than speculative. (Sec. 4.1–4.2.)

### Deeper Analysis Needed
1. **Define the estimand of “fair comparison”** (default setting vs tuned envelope vs tuning-budget-constrained deployment), then justify why Best-of-N is the right decision criterion for the paper’s refutation claim. Without this, the “superiority vanishes” conclusion is under-scoped. (Sec. 3; Sec. 6 lesson #1.)

### Visualizations & Case Studies
1. **Reproducibility bundle inside the paper (or appendix):** exact run IDs/config grids; archived links/screenshots for the “Telegram” evidence; scripts for recomputing Table 1 and the alleged 7.80→5.80 correction. Without embedding this, the strongest claims remain vulnerable. (Secs. 2.4, 4.3.)

### Obvious Next Steps
1. **Turn the “blueprint” into an explicit, reusable protocol** (checklist + required artifacts + statistical guidance + tuning-budget reporting standard). Without that, the main contribution is a case study, not a blueprint. (Abstract; Sec. 6.)

## Overall Assessment

The case study likely contains real and important critiques (especially around tuning-budget fairness and human-eval statistics), but in its current form it overreaches relative to what it rigorously substantiates inside the document: several of the most serious accusations (selective reporting, misreporting, retractions, omitted data confirmed by authors) are not documented with publication-grade, auditable evidence, and the claimed “blueprint” is not operationalized beyond general advice plus one analysis pattern. As written, I would not recommend acceptance as an ICLR research paper; it could become publishable if it (i) hardens the evidentiary chain for the integrity-critical claims, (ii) precisely defines and justifies its fairness/estimand choices, and (iii) upgrades the “blueprint” from rhetoric to a concrete, reusable standard.
### Strength Finder
## Strengths

### Core strengths (directly support the paper's main claims)
1. **Demonstrates that the original min‑p human-eval claim fails under correct, condition-wise testing and multiple-comparisons control**: Sec. 2.2 + Table 1 re-run the hypothesis tests per metric×temperature×baseline (12 one-sided paired t-tests) and show that after Bonferroni correction only **1/12** comparisons remains significant (and **0/12** at α=0.01), directly undermining the “consistently … across all settings” claim using the original paper’s own data.
2. **Provides concrete evidence of material data omission that changes conclusions, backed by an audit trail**: Sec. 2.1 documents discovery (and public confirmation with the original authors) that **basic sampling** human-eval scores—**~1/3 of all collected scores**—were excluded from the original methodology/results, then later added to the camera-ready table without updating conclusions; this is a specific, consequential transparency failure supporting the paper’s rigor-crisis case study thesis.
3. **Introduces and executes a fair-comparison methodology that controls for “hyperparameter volume,” directly testing cherry-picking risk**: Sec. 3 defines two “Best-of-N” style analyses (best achievable score vs tuning budget; and min‑p’s margin vs best alternative under equal N), and Fig. 4–5 report that **min‑p becomes largely indistinguishable** from other samplers when equalizing hyperparameter search—evidence for the blueprint lesson “control for tuning volume.”
4. **Backs the “rigor blueprint” with a large-scale, resource-intensive replication that is hard to dismiss as underpowered**: Sec. 3 details a sweep over **9 models × 2 stages × 4 samplers × 31 temperatures × 6 hyperparameters × 3 seeds** (plus repeats for subsampling), costing **~6000 A100-hours**, substantially exceeding typical single-setting benchmark checks and providing strong empirical support for the paper’s fairness/tuning-volume argument.

### Supporting strengths
1. **Triangulates across multiple evaluation modalities and shows how each can mislead without rigor controls**: The paper doesn’t rely on one critique; it audits **human evals (Sec. 2), benchmark sweeps (Sec. 3), LLM-as-a-judge (Sec. 4), and adoption metrics (Sec. 5)**, and in each case points to a *specific* failure mode (e.g., pooled t-test across conditions in Sec. 2.2; indirect judge design in Sec. 4.1), supporting the broader “blueprint” framing with diverse concrete examples.
2. **Uses uncertainty-aware visualization to rebut overconfident claims from point estimates**: Sec. 2.2 cites Fig. 1’s **95% confidence intervals** frequently overlapping across samplers, and Sec. 4.2 adds **95% CIs on win rates** in Fig. 6 (right), strengthening the argument that reported differences may be practically/statistically indistinguishable when uncertainty is shown.
3. **Provides a principled alternative to the original paper’s “consistent across settings” wording via an appropriate global test**: Sec. 2.2 explicitly motivates and applies an **Intersection–Union Test** for the “better in all conditions” claim, using the maximum p-value (0.378) to show the global claim is unsupported—this is more aligned to the stated claim than the original pooled test.
4. **Produces evidence of inconsistent reporting in judge evals using the original authors’ shared artifacts**: Sec. 4.3 ties Table 3(b)’s reported win rates to a publicly shared link and shows a pattern of selecting **the higher of two min‑p scores but the lower of two top‑p scores**, a concrete example supporting the paper’s “selective/inconsistent reporting” lesson.
5. **Verifies and documents a retraction of high-impact adoption claims and explains why it matters for peer review**: Sec. 5 shows attempted verification of “54k repos / 1.1M stars,” contrasts with a computed upper bound from major LM repos (**453k stars total as of March 2025**), and notes the original authors **retracted** both numbers; it further ties this to reviewer justifications (Sec. 5), supporting the blueprint lesson to scrutinize headline claims that can sway acceptance.

### Presentation / practical strengths (optional)
1. **Actionable checklist-style lessons grounded in earlier sections’ empirical findings**: Sec. 6 enumerates six practices (tuning-volume control, transparent stats incl. multiple comparisons, full data release, scrutinize qualitative summaries and bold claims, methodological clarity, consistent reporting), each explicitly linked back to a demonstrated failure in Secs. 2–5, making the “blueprint” practically usable rather than purely editorial.

## Summary

The paper presents a rigor “blueprint” by auditing a prominent min‑p sampling paper across human evaluation, benchmark experiments, LLM-judge studies, and adoption claims, arguing the original superiority conclusions are not supported when analyses are done correctly and fairly. The single strongest piece of evidence is the human-evaluation reanalysis in **Sec. 2.2 / Table 1**, which directly tests the original “consistently across all settings” claim using the original data and shows that after proper per-condition testing and **Bonferroni correction**, essentially none of the claimed advantages remain.

--- Merged Review ---
## Summary
This paper presents a “blueprint” for more rigorous empirical ML via an in-depth audit of a single high-visibility prior work on **min‑p sampling**, reanalyzing four evidence streams (human evals, NLP benchmarks, LLM-as-a-judge, and adoption claims) and arguing the original paper’s central superiority claim is **not supported** by its own data and extensions run with the authors’ code.

## Strengths
- **Human-eval reanalysis directly targets the original “consistently across all settings” claim with per-condition tests + multiple-comparison control.** Table 1 reports **12 one-sided paired t-tests** and notes that after Bonferroni, only **1/12** is significant at α=0.05 (and **0/12** at α=0.01), and adds an IUT framing for “better in all settings” (Sec. 2.2, Table 1, lines 25–51).
- **Concrete, large-scale tuning-budget sensitivity analysis (“Best-of-N”) on GSM8K-CoT.** The paper runs a sizable sweep (9 models × base/instruct × 4 samplers × 31 temperatures × 6 sampler hyperparams × 3 seeds) and then subsamples configs to compare maxima under equalized “hyperparameter volume” (Sec. 3, lines 84–113), concluding min‑p becomes “largely indistinguishable” when tuning volume is equalized (Sec. 3.1 narrative around Figs. 4–5, lines 116–120).

## Weaknesses

### Fatal
None.

### Major
- **The “blueprint” is mostly rhetorical rather than an instantiated, reusable protocol.** Despite headline claims (“provides a blueprint,” “develop a novel methodology,” Abstract/Intro), what’s delivered is primarily a case study plus a short list of lessons (Sec. 6) without an operational artifact (e.g., checklist with required evidence, auditing procedure, or standardized reporting template). This weakens the paper’s *claimed* general contribution beyond “here is what we did on this case study.” (Abstract/Intro lines 1–16; Sec. 6 lines 147–159)
- **Several integrity-critical claims rely on off-paper or weakly specified provenance, reducing the evidentiary standard for serious allegations.** Examples in the submission text include “We publicly confirmed with the authors” for omitted baseline data (Sec. 2.1, line 23), a “Telegram link” as key evidence for inconsistent reporting (Sec. 4.3, lines 134–136), and statements like “ongoing work to publish” when describing judge-eval data scrutiny (Sec. 4.2, line 130). Given the paper’s repeated “invalidated” framing (Abstract line 1; Sec. 6 line 145), the strongest accusations would need more self-contained, publication-grade documentation within the paper/appendix (archived links/snapshots, run IDs, exact computations).
- **Fairness/estimand mismatch risk in the “Best-of-N controls hyperparameter volume” framing.** The Best-of-N analysis is a reasonable *tuning-budget* lens, but the paper treats it as the fairness criterion without clearly defining the estimand being adjudicated (default behavior vs envelope under tuning vs operational budget). As written, the evidence cleanly supports a narrower claim (“under equalized sampled configuration budget on GSM8K-CoT, min‑p is not better”), but the paper’s language is broader (“invalidates its central claim,” “across benchmarks,” Abstract lines 1–16; Sec. 3 line 85). Tightening scope/estimand would make the conclusions better aligned to the evidence.

### Minor
- **Some statistical/annotation details needed to fully validate the reanalyses are not provided in the extracted main text.** For example, Sec. 2.2 relies on paired one-sided t-tests but does not spell out pairing unit, handling of exclusions, or assumption checks; Sec. 2.3 reports “manual annotation” of qualitative responses but does not report agreement metrics or coding rubric in the visible text (Sec. 2.2 lines 47–51; Sec. 2.3 lines 59–63).
- **A serious numeric-misreport allegation is stated without enough in-paper detail.** The claim that a reported mean “7.80” should be “5.80” is made in one sentence (Sec. 2.4, lines 79–80) without showing the calculation or the exact rows used (in the extracted content), which is important given the accusation’s severity.

### Trivial
None.

## Nice-to-Haves
- Add one decisive **direct** LLM-judge comparison design (min‑p vs top‑p under the same judge/prompt set) to complement the non-transitivity/indirect-comparison critique (Sec. 4.1, lines 124–127).

## Removed Points
These points are flagged to be removed, treat them with caution.
- **“Best-of-N uses N=1..100 but only 6 hyperparameters” confusion** — The paper is clear that it subsamples “hyperparameters/configurations” from a broader grid including **31 temperatures × 6 sampler values × 3 seeds** (Sec. 3, lines 92–113). While terminology could be clarified, the basic feasibility is not a substantive flaw.
- **Criticism that the IUT framing is necessarily a strawman** — The paper explicitly quotes the original claim as “consistently… across all settings” and motivates IUT as appropriate for “better in all 12 comparisons” (Sec. 2.2, lines 27–51). Whether “consistently” should be interpreted statistically is debatable, but the paper does anchor the interpretation in quoted language.

## Novel Insights
The strongest meta-scientific contribution here is not any single re-test, but the demonstration that *evaluation-degree-of-freedom asymmetries* (pooled vs per-condition testing; unequal tuning volume; indirect judge graphs; selective choice among multiple runs) can systematically manufacture apparent gains even when all underlying components are “standard.” The paper would be substantially stronger if it elevated this into an explicit, reusable auditing protocol with required artifacts and a clear estimand taxonomy, so future audits do not depend on bespoke investigative work.

## Suggestions
- Convert Sec. 6 into an explicit **audit protocol**: (i) define target estimands (default / tuned-budget / frontier), (ii) require reporting of tuning budget and selection rules, (iii) require archived evidence for any integrity-sensitive claim (run IDs, immutable links/snapshots), and (iv) provide a minimal checklist others can apply.
- For every serious allegation (omitted data, inconsistent reporting, numeric misreport), include a self-contained “evidence box” with exact pointers (dataset fields, computation, and archival reference) so the paper itself carries the burden of proof.
- Narrow and standardize the paper’s headline claims to match the evidence actually produced (e.g., explicitly: “GSM8K-CoT under equalized configuration budget”; “human eval high-diversity condition”).

## Score and Decision
**Calibration: unavailable.** The required `calibration_search` tool calls failed with an API error (“gpt-5.2-mini is not a valid model ID”), so I could not retrieve high/medium/low-scoring anchors from the provided human-review corpus. My score below is therefore uncalibrated relative to that dataset.

On merit: the paper tackles an important question (rigorous evaluation and scientific auditing) and contains substantive reanalysis and a large benchmark sweep, but it currently overreaches on (i) “blueprint” generality and (ii) evidentiary standards for its most serious accusations. That combination pushes it below the bar for an ICLR research-paper acceptance in its present form.

